var tafsirData_27 = {
  "1": "\"Ṭâ-Sĩn. These are the verses of the Quran; the clear Book\" opens the chapter of the ant, and the verse is the teaching on the opening, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the two parts: \"Ṭâ-Sĩn. These are the verses of the Quran; the clear Book\", and the word \"clear\" is 'mubin,' and the verse joins the letters and the description, and the commentators explain the meaning: the disconnected letters are the chapter's opening signature, and the clear Book is the verses' name, and the two are the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the opening, and the teaching is that the letters of the language are the Book's letters, and the clarity is the Book's own witness, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the guide, and the guide is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the opening, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the Book, and the teaching is that the clear Book is the guide and the good news, and the two are the chapter's next verses, and the next verses open the chapter's description of its own receivers, and the description is the chapter's word. The opening is the chapter's picture of the Book's standing, and the standing is the teaching, and the teaching is that the letters and the clarity are the one announcement, and the announcement is the chapter's word, and the word carries the chapter to its description of the believers, and the description is the chapter's next teaching. The letters and the clear Book are the one announcement, and the announcement teaches that the Book's clarity is its own witness, and the witness opens the chapter's description of its receivers.",
  "2": "\"˹It is˺ a guide and good news for the believers:\" continues the description, and the verse is the teaching on the guide, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse continues: \"˹It is˺ a guide and good news for the believers:\" and the word \"guide\" is 'huda,' and the word \"good news\" is 'bushra,' and the verse names the Book's two faces, and the commentators explain the meaning: the guide is the road, and the good news is the reward, and the two are the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the guide, and the teaching is that the Book leads and promises, and the verse then leaves the sentence to the next verse, and the leaving is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the believers, and the believers are the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the guide, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the receivers, and the teaching is that the Book's two faces are the believers', and the believers' is the chapter's word. The two names are the chapter's picture of the Book's work, and the work is the teaching, and the teaching is that the guided and the promised are the one company, and the company is the chapter's word, and the word carries the description to its detail, and the detail is the chapter's next teaching. The two faces of the guide and the good news are the Book's work, and the work teaches that the guided and the promised are the one company, and the company is the believers'. The two names of the guide and the good news are the Book's own description, and the description teaches that the led and the promised are the one company of the believers.",
  "3": "\"˹those˺ who establish prayer, pay alms-tax, and have sure faith in the Hereafter\" completes the description, and the verse is the teaching on the believers, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse completes the description: \"˹those˺ who establish prayer, pay alms-tax, and have sure faith in the Hereafter\", and the word \"establish\" is 'yuqimun,' and the verse names the three marks, and the commentators explain the meaning: the prayer and the alms and the certainty are the believers' three, and the three are the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the believers, and the teaching is that the Book's receivers are known by the three, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the deniers, and the deniers are the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the believers, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the certainty, and the teaching is that the sure faith in the Hereafter is the three's ground, and the ground is the chapter's word. The three marks are the chapter's picture of the Book's receivers, and the receivers are the teaching, and the teaching is that the prayer and the alms and the certainty are the Book's fruit, and the fruit is the chapter's word, and the word carries the chapter to its contrast, and the contrast is the chapter's next teaching. The three marks of the prayer and the alms and the certainty are the Book's fruit, and the fruit teaches that the sure faith in the Hereafter is the three's ground. The three marks are the believers' description, and the description teaches that the sure faith in the Hereafter is the three's ground, for the certainty carries the prayer and the alms.",
  "4": "\"As for those who do not believe in the Hereafter, We have certainly made their ˹evil˺ deeds appealing to them, so they wander blindly\" states the contrast, and the verse is the teaching on the appealing, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the contrast: \"As for those who do not believe in the Hereafter, We have certainly made their ˹evil˺ deeds appealing to them, so they wander blindly\", and the word \"appealing\" is 'zayyanna,' and the verse records the making-beautiful, and the commentators explain the meaning: the denier's own deeds are made beautiful to him as the earning of his denial, and the making is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the appealing, and the teaching is that the denied Hereafter makes the evil deed the loved, and the verse then states the result: \"so they wander blindly\", and the result is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the wandering, and the teaching is that the one whose evil is beautified is the one who wanders, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the torment, and the torment is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the appealing, and the word is the chapter's teaching on the justice, and the teaching is that the beautified evil is the denier's own earning, and the earning is the chapter's word, and the word carries the contrast to its verdict, and the verdict is the chapter's next teaching. The making-beautiful of the evil is the verse's justice, and the justice teaches that the beautified evil is the denier's own earning, for the denied Hereafter makes the evil deed the loved.",
  "5": "\"It is they who will suffer a dreadful torment, and in the Hereafter they will ˹truly˺ be the greatest losers\" states the verdict, and the verse is the teaching on the torment, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the verdict: \"It is they who will suffer a dreadful torment, and in the Hereafter they will ˹truly˺ be the greatest losers\", and the word \"dreadful\" is 'su',' and the word \"losers\" is 'akhsarun,' and the verse names the two ends, and the commentators explain the meaning: the dreadful is this world's, and the greatest loss is the next's, and the two are the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the torment, and the teaching is that the denier's two ends are the one loss, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the receiving, and the receiving is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the torment, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the measure, and the teaching is that the beautified evil and the greatest loss are the one road's two ends, and the road is the chapter's word. The verdict is the chapter's picture of the contrast's close, and the close is the teaching, and the teaching is that the two receivers and the two ends are the chapter's opening, and the opening is the chapter's word, and the word carries the chapter to its next word, and the next is the chapter's next teaching. The two ends of the dreadful and the greatest loss are the one road, and the road teaches that the beautified evil and the greatest loss are the denier's two ends.",
  "6": "\"And indeed, you ˹O Prophet˺ are receiving the Quran from the One ˹Who is˺ All-Wise, All-Knowing\" states the source, and the verse is the teaching on the receiving, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the source: \"And indeed, you ˹O Prophet˺ are receiving the Quran from the One ˹Who is˺ All-Wise, All-Knowing\", and the word \"receiving\" is 'latulaqqa,' and the verse records the receiving, and the commentators explain the meaning: the Prophet's receiving is from the Wise and the Knowing, and the two names are the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the receiving, and the teaching is that the Book's source is the two names, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the fire, and the fire is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the receiving, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the source, and the teaching is that the received Book is the Wise's, and the Wise's is the chapter's word. The two names are the chapter's picture of the source's standing, and the standing is the teaching, and the teaching is that the wisdom and the knowledge are the Book's two guardians, and the guardians are the chapter's word, and the word opens the chapter's stories, and the stories are the chapter's next teaching, and the first story is Moses's, and Moses's is the chapter's next verses. The two names of the wisdom and the knowledge are the Book's guardians, and the guardians teach that the received Book is the Wise's, and the Wise's is the source's standing.",
  "7": "“˹Remember˺ when Moses said to his family, “I have spotted a fire. I will either bring you some directions from there, or a burning torch so you may warm yourselves.”” opens the account, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the sighting: or \"ith qala Moosa li-ahlihi innee anastu naran sa-ateekum minha bi-khabarin aw ateekum bi-shihabin qabasin la'allakum tastaloon,\" and the commentators note the sighting, for Moses saw the fire and told the family, and the sighting is the seeking, the hope of the directions or the torch, and the verse is the record of the sighting, the opening that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the fire is the sign, and the verse is the record of the sighting, the opening of the call. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the sighting is the seeking and the fire is the sign, and the verse is the record of the sighting, the opening that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the sighting, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own striving. The believer is taught that the seeking of the means is the duty, and the one who seeks is the one whose striving the Lord values, and the believer who travels for the provision is the believer whose striving the Lord accepts, and the sighting of Moses is the teaching, the declaration that the seeking is the thing that the believer must do, and the believer who reads the sighting is the believer whose striving the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the seeking of the means: Moses sought the torch and found the call, and the believer who seeks the means is the believer whose striving the Lord values, and the sighting is the teaching that the seeking is the path of the guidance. The verse is the record of the sighting, and the teaching of the striving that the seeking requires.",
  "8": "“But when he came to it, he was called ˹by Allah˺, “Blessed is the one at the fire, and whoever is around it! Glory be to Allah, the Lord of all worlds.”” records the call, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the call: or \"fa-lamma ja'aha noodiya an boorika man fi an-nari wa man hawlahu wa subhana Allahi rabbi al-'alameen,\" and the commentators note the call, for the Lord called Moses and declared the blessing, and the call is the revelation, the beginning of the mission, and the verse is the record of the call, the opening that the following verse continues, and the commentators note the significance, for the blessing is the honour, and the glorification is the purity, and the verse is the record of the call, the revelation of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the call is the revelation and the blessing is the honour, and the verse is the record of the call, the opening that the following verse continues.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the call, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own reverence. The believer is taught that the blessing of the Lord is the honour, and the one who is called is the one whose reverence the Lord values, and the believer who glorifies the Lord is the believer whose reverence the Lord accepts, and the call to Moses is the teaching, the declaration that the glorification is the thing that the believer must make, and the believer who reads the call is the believer whose reverence the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the reverence of the presence: Moses was called and blessed, and the believer who glorifies the Lord is the believer whose reverence the Lord values, and the call is the teaching that the glorification is the thing that the believer must make. The verse is the record of the call, and the teaching of the reverence that the presence requires.",
  "9": "“O Moses! It is truly I. I am Allah—the Almighty, All-Wise.”” records the declaration, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the declaration: or \"ya Moosa innahu ana Allahu al-'azeezu al-hakeem,\" and the commentators note the declaration, for the Lord declared the identity and the two names, and the declaration is the revelation, the statement of the Lord, and the verse is the record of the declaration, the opening that the following verse commands, and the commentators note the significance, for the declaration is the identity, and the two names are the attributes, and the verse is the record of the declaration, the revelation of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the declaration is the identity and the two names are the attributes, and the verse is the record of the declaration, the opening that the following verse commands.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the declaration, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own certainty. The believer is taught that the declaration of the Lord is the identity, and the one who hears is the one whose certainty the Lord values, and the believer who knows the two names is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the declaration to Moses is the teaching, the declaration that the two names are the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the declaration is the believer whose certainty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the two names: the Lord is the Almighty and the All-Wise, and the believer who knows the two is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the declaration is the teaching that the identity is the thing that the believer must know. The verse is the record of the declaration, and the teaching of the certainty that the two names produce.",
  "10": "“Now, throw down your staff!” But when he saw it slithering like a snake, he ran away without looking back. ˹Allah reassured him,˺ “O Moses! Do not be afraid! Messengers should have no fear in My presence.”” records the command and the reassurance, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the command: or \"wa alqi 'asaka fa-lamma ra'aha tahtazzu ka-annaha jannun walla mudbiran wa lam yu'aqqib ya Moosa la takhaf innee la yakhafu ladayya al-mursaloon,\" and the commentators note the command, for the Lord commanded the throwing and the staff became the snake, and the verse is the record of the command, the sign that the reassurance completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the transformation is the sign, and the fear is the human, and the verse is the record of the command, the sign and the reassurance of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the command is the sign and the reassurance is the comfort, and the verse is the record of the command, the sign that the following verse qualifies.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the reassurance, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own trust. The believer is taught that the reassurance of the Lord is the comfort, and the one who fears is the one whose fear the Lord removes, and the believer who trusts the presence is the believer whose trust the Lord values, and the reassurance to Moses is the teaching, the declaration that the presence is the safety, and the believer who reads the reassurance is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the presence of the Lord: Moses feared and was reassured, and the believer who trusts the presence is the believer whose fear the Lord removes, and the reassurance is the teaching that the presence is the protection. The verse is the record of the command and the reassurance, and the teaching of the trust that the presence produces.",
  "11": "“˹Fear is˺ only for those who do wrong. But if they later mend ˹their˺ evil ˹ways˺ with good, then I am certainly All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.”” records the qualification, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the qualification: or \"illa man zalama thumma baddala husnan ba'da soo'in fa-innee ghafoorun raheem,\" and the commentators note the qualification, for the fear is for the wrongdoer, and the verse is the record of the qualification, the teaching that the forgiveness follows the repentance, and the commentators note the significance, for the exception is the mercy, and the verse is the record of the qualification, the teaching of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the qualification is the teaching and the forgiveness is the mercy, and the verse is the record of the qualification, the teaching that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the qualification, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own hope. The believer is taught that the fear is for the wrongdoer, and the one who mends is the one whose forgiveness the Lord grants, and the believer who repents is the believer whose hope the Lord fulfills, and the qualification of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the mercy is the thing that the Lord grants, and the believer who reads the qualification is the believer whose hope the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the mercy of the repentance: the wrong is feared and the mending is forgiven, and the believer who repents is the believer whose forgiveness the Lord grants, and the qualification is the teaching that the mercy is the thing that the Lord grants. The verse is the record of the qualification, and the teaching of the hope that the mercy carries.",
  "12": "“Now put your hand through ˹the opening of˺ your collar, it will come out ˹shining˺ white, unblemished. ˹These are two˺ of nine signs for Pharaoh and his people. They have truly been a rebellious people.”” records the second sign, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the sign: or \"wa adkhil yadaka fee jaybika takhruj bayda'a min ghayri soo'in fee tis'i ayatin ila Fir'awna wa qawmihi innahum kanoo qawman fasiqeen,\" and the commentators note the sign, for the hand shone white, and the verse is the record of the sign, the second of the nine, and the commentators note the significance, for the white hand is the light, and the nine signs are the proofs, and the verse is the record of the sign, the second sign. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the sign is the light and the nine are the proofs, and the verse is the record of the sign, the second of the nine signs.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the sign, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own certainty. The believer is taught that the signs of the Lord are the proofs, and the one who receives is the one whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the believer who trusts the power is the believer whose trust the Lord values, and the sign of Moses is the teaching, the declaration that the power of the Lord is the complete, and the believer who reads the sign is the believer whose certainty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the power of the Lord: the hand shone white, and the believer who trusts the power is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the sign is the teaching that the light is the easy for the Lord. The verse is the record of the sign, and the teaching of the certainty that the power produces.",
  "13": "“But when Our enlightening signs came to them, they said, “This is pure magic.”” records the rejection, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the rejection: or \"fa-lamma ja'athum ayatuna mubsiratan qaloo hatha sihrun mubeen,\" and the commentators note the rejection, for the people called the signs the magic, and the rejection is the label, the charge of the sorcery, and the verse is the record of the rejection, the label that the following verse exposes, and the commentators note the significance, for the label is the oldest of the charges, and the verse is the record of the rejection, the label of the people. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the rejection is the label and the magic is the charge, and the verse is the record of the rejection, the label that the following verse exposes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the rejection, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own patience. The believer is taught that the label of the magic is the thing that the falsehood uses, and the one who is labelled is the one whose patience the Lord values, and the believer who bears the label is the believer whose patience the Lord accepts, and the rejection of the people is the teaching, the declaration that the label is the thing that the truth faces, and the believer who reads the rejection is the believer whose patience the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the patience of the label: Moses was labelled and prevailed, and the believer who bears the label is the believer whose patience the Lord values, and the rejection is the teaching that the label is the thing that the truth always faces. The verse is the record of the rejection, and the teaching of the patience that the label requires.",
  "14": "“And, although their hearts were convinced the signs were true, they still denied them wrongfully and arrogantly. See then what was the end of the corruptors!”” records the denial, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the denial: or \"wa jahadoo biha wastayqanatha anfusuhum zulman wa 'uluwwan fanzur kayfa kana 'aqibatu al-mufsideen,\" and the commentators note the denial, for the people denied with the conviction, and the denial is the rejection, the denial of the known, and the verse is the record of the denial, the rejection that the following verse reflects upon, and the commentators note the significance, for the denial with the conviction is the gravest of the sins, and the verse is the record of the denial, the rejection of the people. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the denial is the rejection and the conviction is the knowledge, and the verse is the record of the denial, the rejection that the following verse reflects upon.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the denial, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own certainty. The believer is taught that the denial of the known is the gravest, and the one who denies is the one whose denial the Lord weighs, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the denial of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the rejection is the path of the ruin, and the believer who reads the denial is the believer whose certainty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the end of the denial: the people denied and were destroyed, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the denial is the teaching that the rejection is the path of the ruin. The verse is the record of the denial, and the teaching of the certainty that the judgment produces.",
  "15": "“Indeed, We granted knowledge to David and Solomon. And they said ˹in acknowledgment˺, “All praise is for Allah Who has privileged us over many of His faithful servants.”” opens the account of Solomon, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the grant: or \"wa laqad atayna Dawooda wa Sulaymana 'ilman wa qala al-hamdu lillahi allathee faddalana 'ala katheerin min 'ibadihi al-mu'mineen,\" and the commentators note the grant, for the Lord granted the knowledge and the two praised, and the verse is the record of the grant, the opening that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the knowledge is the gift, and the praise is the gratitude, and the verse is the record of the grant, the opening of the account. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the grant is the gift and the praise is the gratitude, and the verse is the record of the grant, the opening that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the grant, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own gratitude. The believer is taught that the knowledge of the Lord is the gift, and the one who praises is the one whose gratitude the Lord values, and the believer who thanks the gift is the believer whose gratitude the Lord accepts, and the grant to the two is the teaching, the declaration that the gratitude is the thing that the believer must offer, and the believer who reads the grant is the believer whose gratitude the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the gratitude of the gift: the two praised the Lord, and the believer who thanks the gift is the believer whose gratitude the Lord values, and the grant is the teaching that the gratitude is the thing that the believer must offer. The verse is the record of the grant, and the teaching of the gratitude that the knowledge requires.",
  "16": "“And David was succeeded by Solomon, who said, “O people! We have been taught the language of birds, and been given everything ˹we need˺. This is indeed a great privilege.”” records the succession, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the succession: or \"wa waritha Sulaymanu Dawooda wa qala ya ayyuha an-nasu 'ullimna mantiqa at-tayri wa ooteena min kulli shay'in inna hatha la-huwa al-fadlu al-mubeen,\" and the commentators note the succession, for Solomon succeeded David, and the verse is the record of the succession, the declaration that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the language of the birds is the gift, and the privilege is the favour, and the verse is the record of the succession, the declaration of Solomon. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the succession is the inheritance and the language is the gift, and the verse is the record of the succession, the declaration that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the succession, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own gratitude. The believer is taught that the inheritance of the knowledge is the gift, and the one who declares is the one whose gratitude the Lord values, and the believer who thanks the favour is the believer whose gratitude the Lord accepts, and the succession of Solomon is the teaching, the declaration that the gratitude is the thing that the believer must offer, and the believer who reads the succession is the believer whose gratitude the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the gratitude of the favour: Solomon declared the privilege, and the believer who thanks the favour is the believer whose gratitude the Lord values, and the succession is the teaching that the gratitude is the thing that the believer must offer. The verse is the record of the succession, and the teaching of the gratitude that the favour requires.",
  "17": "“Solomon’s forces of jinn, humans, and birds were rallied for him, perfectly organized.”” records the army, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the army: or \"wa hushira li-Sulaymana junooduhu mina al-jinni wal-insi wat-tayri fa-hum yoza'oon,\" and the commentators note the army, for the forces were rallied, and the verse is the record of the army, the gathering that the following verse describes, and the commentators note the significance, for the three hosts are the gift, and the organization is the order, and the verse is the record of the army, the gathering of Solomon. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the army is the gift and the organization is the order, and the verse is the record of the army, the gathering that the following verse describes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the army, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own trust. The believer is taught that the power of the Lord is the thing that the believer trusts, and the one whom the Lord grants is the one whose trust the Lord values, and the believer who trusts the power is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the army of Solomon is the teaching, the declaration that the power is the thing that the Lord grants, and the believer who reads the army is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the power of the Lord: the three hosts were rallied, and the believer who trusts the power is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the army is the teaching that the power is the gift. The verse is the record of the army, and the teaching of the trust that the power produces.",
  "18": "“And when they came across a valley of ants, an ant warned, “O ants! Go quickly into your homes so Solomon and his armies do not crush you, unknowingly.”” records the ant, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the ant: or \"hatta itha ataw 'ala wadi an-namli qalat namlatun ya ayyuha an-namlu udkhuloo masakinakum la yahtimannakum Sulaymanu wa junooduhu wa hum la yash'uroon,\" and the commentators note the ant, for the ant warned the ants, and the verse is the record of the ant, the wonder that the following verse reflects upon, and the commentators note the significance, for the speech of the ant is the sign, and the verse is the record of the ant, the wonder of Solomon. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the ant is the sign and the speech is the wonder, and the verse is the record of the ant, the wonder that the following verse reflects upon.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the ant, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own mercy. The believer is taught that the mercy of the ant is the lesson, and the one who reflects is the one whose mercy the Lord values, and the believer who cares for the small is the believer whose mercy the Lord accepts, and the ant of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the mercy is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the ant is the believer whose mercy the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the mercy of the small: the ant cared for the ants, and the believer who cares for the weak is the believer whose mercy the Lord values, and the ant is the teaching that the mercy is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the ant, and the teaching of the mercy that the wonder displays.",
  "19": "“So Solomon smiled in amusement at her words, and prayed, “My Lord! Inspire me to ˹always˺ be thankful for Your favours which You have blessed me and my parents with, and to do good deeds that please you. Admit me, by Your mercy, into ˹the company of˺ Your righteous servants.”” records the prayer, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the prayer: or \"fa-tabassama dahikan min qawliha wa qala rabbi awzi'nee an ashkura ni'mataka allatee an'amta 'alayya wa 'ala walidayya wa an a'mala salihan tardahu wa adkhilnee bi-rahmatika fee 'ibadika as-saliheen,\" and the commentators note the prayer, for Solomon smiled and prayed the gratitude, and the prayer is the gratitude, the seeking of the thanks, and the verse is the record of the prayer, the gratitude that the following verse continues, and the commentators note the significance, for the smile is the amusement, and the prayer is the gratitude, and the verse is the record of the prayer, the gratitude of Solomon. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the prayer is the gratitude and the smile is the amusement, and the verse is the record of the prayer, the gratitude that the following verse continues.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the prayer, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own gratitude. The believer is taught that the gratitude of the favour is the duty, and the one who prays is the one whose gratitude the Lord values, and the believer who thanks the favour is the believer whose gratitude the Lord accepts, and the prayer of Solomon is the teaching, the declaration that the gratitude is the thing that the believer must seek, and the believer who reads the prayer is the believer whose gratitude the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the prayer of the gratitude: Solomon prayed the thanks, and the believer who thanks the favour is the believer whose gratitude the Lord values, and the prayer is the teaching that the gratitude is the thing that the believer must seek. The verse is the record of the prayer, and the teaching of the gratitude that the favour requires.",
  "20": "“˹One day˺ he inspected the birds, and wondered, “Why is it that I cannot see the hoopoe? Or could he be absent?”” records the inspection, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the inspection: or \"wa tafaqqada at-tayra fa-qala ma liya la ara al-hudhuda am kana mina al-gha'ibeen,\" and the commentators note the inspection, for Solomon inspected the birds, and the inspection is the care, the notice of the absence, and the verse is the record of the inspection, the care that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the inspection is the care, and the verse is the record of the inspection, the care of Solomon. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the inspection is the care and the absence is the notice, and the verse is the record of the inspection, the care that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the inspection, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own care. The believer is taught that the care of the duty is the thing that the believer must hold, and the one who inspects is the one whose care the Lord values, and the believer who attends to the trust is the believer whose care the Lord accepts, and the inspection of Solomon is the teaching, the declaration that the care is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the inspection is the believer whose care the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the care of the trust: Solomon inspected the birds, and the believer who attends to the trust is the believer whose care the Lord values, and the inspection is the teaching that the care is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the inspection, and the teaching of the care that the trust requires.",
  "21": "“I will surely subject him to a severe punishment, or ˹even˺ slaughter him, unless he brings me a compelling excuse.”” records the threat, and the commentators note the two parts of the threat. The verse records the threat: or \"la-u'aththibannahu 'athaban shadeedan aw la-athbahannahu aw la-ya'tiyanee bi-sultanin mubeen,\" and the commentators note the threat, for Solomon threatened the hoopoe, and the threat is the discipline, the demand of the excuse, and the verse is the record of the threat, the discipline that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the threat is the discipline, and the verse is the record of the threat, the demand of Solomon. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the threat is the discipline and the excuse is the demand, and the verse is the record of the threat, the discipline that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the threat, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own justice. The believer is taught that the discipline of the trust is the justice, and the one who threatens is the one whose justice the Lord values, and the believer who holds the trust is the believer whose justice the Lord accepts, and the threat of Solomon is the teaching, the declaration that the discipline is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the threat is the believer whose justice the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the discipline of the trust: Solomon threatened the hoopoe, and the believer who holds the trust is the believer whose justice the Lord values, and the threat is the teaching that the discipline is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the threat, and the teaching of the justice that the trust requires.",
  "22": "“It was not long before the bird came and said, “I have found out something you do not know. I have just come to you from Sheba with sure news.”” records the return, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the return: or \"fa-makatha ghayra ba'eedin fa-qala ahattu bima lam tuhit bihi wa ji'tuka min saba'in bi-naba'in yaqeen,\" and the commentators note the return, for the hoopoe returned with the news, and the return is the answer, the bringing of the tidings, and the verse is the record of the return, the answer that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the news is the tidings, and the verse is the record of the return, the answer of the hoopoe. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the return is the answer and the news is the tidings, and the verse is the record of the return, the answer that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the return, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own honesty. The believer is taught that the excuse is the thing that the trust demands, and the one who returns is the one whose honesty the Lord values, and the believer who brings the truth is the believer whose honesty the Lord accepts, and the return of the hoopoe is the teaching, the declaration that the honesty is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the return is the believer whose honesty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the honesty of the return: the hoopoe brought the news, and the believer who brings the truth is the believer whose honesty the Lord values, and the return is the teaching that the honesty is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the return, and the teaching of the honesty that the trust requires.",
  "23": "“Indeed, I found a woman ruling over them, who has been given everything ˹she needs˺, and who has a magnificent throne.”” records the report, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the report: or \"innee wajadtu imra'atan tamlikuhum wa ootiyat min kulli shay'in wa laha 'arshun 'azeem,\" and the commentators note the report, for the hoopoe reported the queen, and the report is the news, the description of Sheba, and the verse is the record of the report, the news that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the queen is the ruler, and the throne is the power, and the verse is the record of the report, the news of the hoopoe. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the report is the news and the queen is the ruler, and the verse is the record of the report, the news that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the report, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own discernment. The believer is taught that the report of the truth is the duty, and the one who reports is the one whose honesty the Lord values, and the believer who brings the news is the believer whose honesty the Lord accepts, and the report of the hoopoe is the teaching, the declaration that the honesty is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the report is the believer whose honesty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the honesty of the report: the hoopoe reported the truth, and the believer who brings the news is the believer whose honesty the Lord values, and the report is the teaching that the honesty is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the report, and the teaching of the honesty that the trust requires.",
  "24": "“I found her and her people prostrating to the sun instead of Allah. For Satan has made their deeds appealing to them—hindering them from the ˹Right˺ Way and leaving them unguided—”” records the description, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the description: or \"wajadtuha wa qawmaha yasjudoona lish-shamsi min dooni Allahi wa zayyana lahumu ash-shaytanu a'malahum fa-saddahum 'ani as-sabeeli fa-hum la yahtadoon,\" and the commentators note the description, for the hoopoe reported the worship, and the description is the news, the report of the idolatry, and the verse is the record of the description, the news that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the worship of the sun is the idolatry, and the beautifying is the deception, and the verse is the record of the description, the news of the hoopoe. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the description is the news and the worship is the idolatry, and the verse is the record of the description, the news that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the description, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own certainty. The believer is taught that the idolatry is the thing that the truth exposes, and the one who reports is the one whose honesty the Lord values, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the description of the hoopoe is the teaching, the declaration that the idolatry is the thing that the truth must confront, and the believer who reads the description is the believer whose certainty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the honesty of the report: the hoopoe reported the idolatry, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the description is the teaching that the idolatry is the thing that the truth must confront. The verse is the record of the description, and the teaching of the certainty that the report produces.",
  "25": "“so they do not prostrate to Allah, Who brings forth what is hidden in the heavens and the earth, and knows what you ˹all˺ conceal and what you reveal.”” completes the description, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse completes the description: or \"alla yasjudoo lillahi allathee yukhriju al-khab'a fi as-samawati wal-ardi wa ya'lamu ma tukhfoona wa ma tu'linoon,\" and the commentators note the completion, for the hoopoe declared the Lord, and the completion is the news, the declaration of the truth, and the verse is the record of the completion, the news that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the declaration is the truth, and the verse is the record of the completion, the news of the hoopoe. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the completion is the news and the declaration is the truth, and the verse is the record of the completion, the news that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the completion, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own certainty. The believer is taught that the declaration of the truth is the duty, and the one who declares is the one whose certainty the Lord values, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the completion of the hoopoe is the teaching, the declaration that the truth is the thing that the believer must declare, and the believer who reads the completion is the believer whose certainty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the declaration of the truth: the hoopoe declared the Lord, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the completion is the teaching that the truth is the thing that the believer must declare. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the certainty that the declaration produces.",
  "26": "“˹He is˺ Allah! There is no god ˹worthy of worship˺ except Him, the Lord of the Mighty Throne.”” completes the news, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse completes the news: or \"Allahu la ilaha illa huwa rabbu al-'arshi al-'azeem,\" and the commentators note the completion, for the hoopoe declared the oneness, and the completion is the news, the statement of the faith, and the verse is the record of the completion, the news that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the oneness is the truth, and the throne is the majesty, and the verse is the record of the completion, the news of the hoopoe. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the completion is the news and the oneness is the truth, and the verse is the record of the completion, the news that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the completion, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own faith. The believer is taught that the oneness is the truth, and the one who declares is the one whose faith the Lord values, and the believer who holds the oneness is the believer whose faith the Lord confirms, and the completion of the hoopoe is the teaching, the declaration that the oneness is the thing that the believer must declare, and the believer who reads the completion is the believer whose faith the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the declaration of the oneness: the hoopoe declared the One, and the believer who holds the oneness is the believer whose faith the Lord confirms, and the completion is the teaching that the oneness is the thing that the believer must declare. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the faith that the declaration establishes.",
  "27": "“Solomon said, “We will see whether you are telling the truth or lying.”” records the reply, and the commentators note the two parts of the reply. The verse records the reply: or \"qala sa-nanzuru a-sadaqta am kunta mina al-kathibeen,\" and the commentators note the reply, for Solomon declared the test, and the reply is the discernment, the verification of the report, and the verse is the record of the reply, the discernment that the following verse commands, and the commentators note the significance, for the test is the discernment, and the verse is the record of the reply, the discernment of Solomon. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the reply is the discernment and the test is the verification, and the verse is the record of the reply, the discernment that the following verse commands.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the reply, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own discernment. The believer is taught that the verification of the report is the discernment, and the one who tests is the one whose discernment the Lord values, and the believer who verifies the truth is the believer whose discernment the Lord accepts, and the reply of Solomon is the teaching, the declaration that the discernment is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the reply is the believer whose discernment the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the discernment of the truth: Solomon tested the report, and the believer who verifies the truth is the believer whose discernment the Lord values, and the reply is the teaching that the discernment is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the reply, and the teaching of the discernment that the verification requires.",
  "28": "“Go with this letter of mine and deliver it to them, then stand aside and see how they will respond.”” records the command, and the commentators note the two parts of the command. The verse records the command: or \"ithhab bi-kitabee hatha fa-alqih ilayhim thumma tawalla 'anhum fanzur matha yarji'oon,\" and the commentators note the command, for Solomon commanded the letter, and the command is the wisdom, the sending of the message, and the verse is the record of the command, the wisdom that the following verse describes, and the commentators note the significance, for the letter is the message, and the standing aside is the observation, and the verse is the record of the command, the wisdom of Solomon. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the command is the wisdom and the letter is the message, and the verse is the record of the command, the wisdom that the following verse describes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the command, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own wisdom. The believer is taught that the message is the thing that the believer must deliver, and the one who sends is the one whose wisdom the Lord values, and the believer who delivers the truth is the believer whose wisdom the Lord accepts, and the command of Solomon is the teaching, the declaration that the wisdom is the thing that the believer must use, and the believer who reads the command is the believer whose wisdom the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the wisdom of the message: Solomon sent the letter, and the believer who delivers the truth is the believer whose wisdom the Lord values, and the command is the teaching that the wisdom is the thing that the believer must use. The verse is the record of the command, and the teaching of the wisdom that the message requires.",
  "29": "“The Queen ˹later˺ announced, “O chiefs! Indeed, a noble letter has been delivered to me.”” records the announcement, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the announcement: or \"qalat ya ayyuha al-mala'u innee ulqiya ilayya kitabun kareem,\" and the commentators note the announcement, for the queen announced the letter, and the announcement is the consultation, the beginning of the counsel, and the verse is the record of the announcement, the consultation that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the letter is the message, and the verse is the record of the announcement, the consultation of the queen. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the announcement is the consultation and the letter is the message, and the verse is the record of the announcement, the consultation that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the announcement, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own consultation. The believer is taught that the consultation is the wisdom, and the one who consults is the one whose wisdom the Lord values, and the believer who seeks the counsel is the believer whose wisdom the Lord accepts, and the announcement of the queen is the teaching, the declaration that the consultation is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the announcement is the believer whose wisdom the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the wisdom of the consultation: the queen consulted the chiefs, and the believer who seeks the counsel is the believer whose wisdom the Lord values, and the announcement is the teaching that the consultation is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the announcement, and the teaching of the wisdom that the consultation requires.",
  "30": "“It is from Solomon, and it reads: ‘In the Name of Allah—the Most Compassionate, Most Merciful.’” records the letter, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the letter: or \"innahu min Sulaymana wa innahu bismi Allahi ar-rahmani ar-raheem,\" and the commentators note the letter, for the letter begins with the name, and the letter is the message, the opening of the correspondence, and the verse is the record of the letter, the message that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the opening with the name is the teaching, and the verse is the record of the letter, the message of Solomon. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the letter is the message and the name is the opening, and the verse is the record of the letter, the message that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the letter, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own speech. The believer is taught that the opening with the name of the Lord is the teaching, and the one who begins is the one whose speech the Lord values, and the believer who opens with the name is the believer whose speech the Lord accepts, and the letter of Solomon is the teaching, the declaration that the name is the thing that the believer must begin with, and the believer who reads the letter is the believer whose speech the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the opening of the speech: Solomon began with the name, and the believer who opens with the name is the believer whose speech the Lord values, and the letter is the teaching that the name is the thing that the believer must begin with. The verse is the record of the letter, and the teaching of the speech that the name requires.",
  "31": "“Do not be arrogant with me, but come to me, fully submitting ˹to Allah˺.’”” completes the letter, and the commentators note the two parts of the completion. The verse completes the letter: or \"alla ta'loo 'alayya wa'toonee muslimeen,\" and the commentators note the completion, for Solomon demanded the submission, and the completion is the message, the command of the truth, and the verse is the record of the completion, the message that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the demand is the call, and the verse is the record of the completion, the message of Solomon. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the completion is the message and the demand is the call, and the verse is the record of the completion, the message that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the completion, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own calling. The believer is taught that the call to the submission is the message, and the one who calls is the one whose calling the Lord values, and the believer who calls to the truth is the believer whose calling the Lord accepts, and the completion of Solomon is the teaching, the declaration that the submission is the thing that the believer must call to, and the believer who reads the completion is the believer whose calling the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the call of the submission: Solomon called the submission, and the believer who calls to the truth is the believer whose calling the Lord values, and the completion is the teaching that the submission is the thing that the believer must call to. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the calling that the message requires.",
  "32": "“She said, “O chiefs! Advise me in this matter of mine, for I would never make any decision without you.”” records the consultation, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the consultation: or \"qalat ya ayyuha al-mala'u aftoonee fee amree ma kuntu qati'atan amran hatta tashhadoon,\" and the commentators note the consultation, for the queen asked the counsel, and the consultation is the wisdom, the seeking of the advice, and the verse is the record of the consultation, the wisdom that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the consultation is the wisdom, and the verse is the record of the consultation, the wisdom of the queen. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the consultation is the wisdom and the counsel is the advice, and the verse is the record of the consultation, the wisdom that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the consultation, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own wisdom. The believer is taught that the consultation is the wisdom, and the one who consults is the one whose wisdom the Lord values, and the believer who seeks the counsel is the believer whose wisdom the Lord accepts, and the consultation of the queen is the teaching, the declaration that the consultation is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the consultation is the believer whose wisdom the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the wisdom of the consultation: the queen asked the counsel, and the believer who seeks the advice is the believer whose wisdom the Lord values, and the consultation is the teaching that the wisdom is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the consultation, and the teaching of the wisdom that the counsel requires.",
  "33": "“They responded, “We are a people of strength and great ˹military˺ might, but the decision is yours, so decide what you will command.”” records the reply, and the commentators note the two parts of the reply. The verse records the reply: or \"qaloo nahnu oloo quwwatin wa oloo ba'sin shadeedin wal-amru ilayki fanzuree matha ta'mureen,\" and the commentators note the reply, for the chiefs declared the strength and deferred the decision, and the reply is the counsel, the statement of the power, and the verse is the record of the reply, the counsel that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the strength is the power, and the deference is the trust, and the verse is the record of the reply, the counsel of the chiefs. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the reply is the counsel and the strength is the power, and the verse is the record of the reply, the counsel that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the reply, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own wisdom. The believer is taught that the counsel of the strength is the thing that the truth weighs, and the one who defers is the one whose trust the Lord values, and the believer who seeks the wisdom is the believer whose wisdom the Lord accepts, and the reply of the chiefs is the teaching, the declaration that the wisdom is the thing that the believer must seek, and the believer who reads the reply is the believer whose wisdom the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the wisdom of the counsel: the chiefs deferred the decision, and the believer who seeks the wisdom is the believer whose wisdom the Lord values, and the reply is the teaching that the wisdom is the thing that the believer must seek. The verse is the record of the reply, and the teaching of the wisdom that the counsel requires.",
  "34": "“She reasoned, “Indeed, when kings invade a land, they ruin it and debase its nobles. They really do so!”” records the reasoning, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the reasoning: or \"qalat inna al-mulooka itha dakhaloo qaryatan afsadooha wa ja'aloo a'izzata ahliha athillatan wa kathalika yaf'aloon,\" and the commentators note the reasoning, for the queen reasoned the destruction of the kings, and the reasoning is the wisdom, the prudence of the ruler, and the verse is the record of the reasoning, the wisdom that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the prudence is the wisdom, and the verse is the record of the reasoning, the wisdom of the queen. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the reasoning is the wisdom and the prudence is the insight, and the verse is the record of the reasoning, the wisdom that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the reasoning, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own prudence. The believer is taught that the prudence is the wisdom, and the one who reasons is the one whose wisdom the Lord values, and the believer who weighs the matters is the believer whose wisdom the Lord accepts, and the reasoning of the queen is the teaching, the declaration that the prudence is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the reasoning is the believer whose wisdom the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the prudence of the ruler: the queen reasoned the destruction, and the believer who weighs the matters is the believer whose wisdom the Lord values, and the reasoning is the teaching that the prudence is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the reasoning, and the teaching of the wisdom that the prudence requires.",
  "35": "“But I will certainly send him a gift, and see what ˹response˺ my envoys will return with.”” records the plan, and the commentators note the two parts of the plan. The verse records the plan: or \"wa innee mursilatun ilayhim bi-hadiyyatin fa-naziratun bima yarji'u al-mursaloon,\" and the commentators note the plan, for the queen planned the gift, and the plan is the wisdom, the strategy of the ruler, and the verse is the record of the plan, the wisdom that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the gift is the strategy, and the verse is the record of the plan, the wisdom of the queen. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the plan is the wisdom and the gift is the strategy, and the verse is the record of the plan, the wisdom that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the plan, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own wisdom. The believer is taught that the strategy of the gift is the wisdom, and the one who plans is the one whose wisdom the Lord values, and the believer who weighs the matters is the believer whose wisdom the Lord accepts, and the plan of the queen is the teaching, the declaration that the wisdom is the thing that the believer must use, and the believer who reads the plan is the believer whose wisdom the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the wisdom of the strategy: the queen planned the gift, and the believer who weighs the matters is the believer whose wisdom the Lord values, and the plan is the teaching that the wisdom is the thing that the believer must use. The verse is the record of the plan, and the teaching of the wisdom that the strategy requires.",
  "36": "“When the chief-envoy came to him, Solomon said, “Do you offer me wealth? What Allah has granted me is far greater than what He has granted you. No! It is you who rejoice in ˹receiving˺ gifts.”” records the reply, and the commentators note the two parts of the reply. The verse records the reply: or \"fa-lamma ja'a Sulaymana qala a-tumiddoonani bi-malin fa-ma ataniya Allahu khayrun mimma atakum bal antum bi-hadiyyatikum tafrahoon,\" and the commentators note the reply, for Solomon refused the gift, and the reply is the detachment, the declaration of the sufficiency, and the verse is the record of the reply, the detachment that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the refusal is the detachment, and the verse is the record of the reply, the detachment of Solomon. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the reply is the detachment and the refusal is the sufficiency, and the verse is the record of the reply, the detachment that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the reply, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own detachment. The believer is taught that the detachment from the wealth is the thing that the believer must hold, and the one who refuses is the one whose detachment the Lord values, and the believer who trusts the provision is the believer whose detachment the Lord accepts, and the reply of Solomon is the teaching, the declaration that the detachment is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the reply is the believer whose detachment the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the detachment of the servant: Solomon refused the gift, and the believer who trusts the provision is the believer whose detachment the Lord values, and the reply is the teaching that the detachment is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the reply, and the teaching of the detachment that the sufficiency requires.",
  "37": "“Go back to them, for we will certainly mobilize against them forces which they can never resist, and we will drive them out from there in disgrace, fully humbled.”” completes the reply, and the commentators note the two parts of the completion. The verse completes the reply: or \"irji' ilayhim fa-la-na'tiyannahum bi-junoodin la qibala lahum biha wa la-nukhrijannahum minha athillatan wa hum saghiroon,\" and the commentators note the completion, for Solomon threatened the mobilization, and the completion is the warning, the declaration of the power, and the verse is the record of the completion, the warning that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the warning is the power, and the verse is the record of the completion, the warning of Solomon. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the completion is the warning and the power is the mobilization, and the verse is the record of the completion, the warning that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the completion, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own confidence. The believer is taught that the confidence of the truth is the thing that the believer must hold, and the one who warns is the one whose confidence the Lord values, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose confidence the Lord confirms, and the completion of Solomon is the teaching, the declaration that the confidence is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the completion is the believer whose confidence the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the confidence of the truth: Solomon warned the envoys, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose confidence the Lord confirms, and the completion is the teaching that the confidence is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the confidence that the truth supplies.",
  "38": "“Solomon asked, “O chiefs! Which of you can bring me her throne before they come to me in ˹full˺ submission?”” records the request, and the commentators note the two parts of the request. The verse records the request: or \"qala ya ayyuha al-mala'u ayyukum ya'teenee bi-'arshiha qabla an ya'toonee muslimeen,\" and the commentators note the request, for Solomon asked the throne, and the request is the test, the demonstration of the power, and the verse is the record of the request, the test that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the request is the test, and the verse is the record of the request, the test of Solomon. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the request is the test and the throne is the object, and the verse is the record of the request, the test that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the request, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own certainty. The believer is taught that the power of the Lord is the thing that the believer trusts, and the one who requests is the one whose certainty the Lord values, and the believer who trusts the power is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the request of Solomon is the teaching, the declaration that the power is the thing that the believer must trust, and the believer who reads the request is the believer whose certainty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the power of the Lord: Solomon asked the throne, and the believer who trusts the power is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the request is the teaching that the power is the thing that the believer must trust. The verse is the record of the request, and the teaching of the certainty that the power produces.",
  "39": "“One mighty jinn responded, “I can bring it to you before you rise from this council of yours. And I am quite strong and trustworthy for this ˹task˺.”” records the reply, and the commentators note the two parts of the reply. The verse records the reply: or \"qala 'ifreetun mina al-jinni ana ateeka bihi qabla an taqooma min maqamika wa innee 'alayhi la-qawiyyun ameen,\" and the commentators note the reply, for the jinn offered the throne, and the reply is the claim, the offer of the power, and the verse is the record of the reply, the claim that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the claim is the offer, and the verse is the record of the reply, the claim of the jinn. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the reply is the claim and the offer is the power, and the verse is the record of the reply, the claim that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the reply, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own certainty. The believer is taught that the power of the creatures is the thing that the believer weighs, and the one who offers is the one whose claim the truth tests, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the reply of the jinn is the teaching, the declaration that the power of the creatures is the thing that the truth weighs, and the believer who reads the reply is the believer whose certainty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the limit of the creatures: the jinn offered the throne, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the reply is the teaching that the power of the creatures is the thing that the truth weighs. The verse is the record of the reply, and the teaching of the certainty that the power produces.",
  "40": "“But the one who had knowledge of the Scripture said, “I can bring it to you in the blink of an eye.” So when Solomon saw it placed before him, he exclaimed, “This is by the grace of my Lord to test me whether I am grateful or ungrateful. And whoever is grateful, it is only for their own good. But whoever is ungrateful, surely my Lord is Self-Sufficient, Most Generous.”” records the miracle, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the miracle: or \"qala allathee 'indahu 'ilmun mina al-kitabi ana ateeka bihi qabla an yartadda ilayka tarfuka fa-lamma ra'ahu mustaqirran 'indahu qala hatha min fadli rabbee li-yabluwanee a-ashkuru am akfuru wa man shakara fa-innama yashkuru li-nafsihi wa man kafara fa-inna rabbee ghaniyyun kareem,\" and the commentators note the miracle, for the throne was brought in the blink, and the verse is the record of the miracle, the demonstration of the power, and the commentators note the significance, for the miracle is the power, and the gratitude is the test, and the verse is the record of the miracle, the demonstration of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the miracle is the power and the gratitude is the test, and the verse is the record of the miracle, the demonstration that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the miracle, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own gratitude. The believer is taught that the gratitude is the test, and the one who is granted is the one whose gratitude the Lord weighs, and the believer who thanks the favour is the believer whose gratitude the Lord values, and the miracle of Solomon is the teaching, the declaration that the gratitude is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the miracle is the believer whose gratitude the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the test of the gratitude: Solomon was granted and tested, and the believer who thanks the favour is the believer whose gratitude the Lord values, and the miracle is the teaching that the gratitude is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the miracle, and the teaching of the gratitude that the favour requires.",
  "41": "“˹Then˺ Solomon said, “Disguise her throne for her so we may see whether she will recognize ˹it˺ or she will not be able to.”” records the test, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the test: or \"qala nakkiroo laha 'arshaha nanzur a-tahtadee am takoonu mina allatheena la yahtadoon,\" and the commentators note the test, for Solomon disguised the throne, and the test is the wisdom, the trial of the queen, and the verse is the record of the test, the wisdom that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the test is the wisdom, and the verse is the record of the test, the wisdom of Solomon. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the test is the wisdom and the disguise is the trial, and the verse is the record of the test, the wisdom that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the test, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own wisdom. The believer is taught that the test of the intellect is the wisdom, and the one who tests is the one whose wisdom the Lord values, and the believer who weighs the matters is the believer whose wisdom the Lord accepts, and the test of Solomon is the teaching, the declaration that the wisdom is the thing that the believer must use, and the believer who reads the test is the believer whose wisdom the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the wisdom of the test: Solomon tested the queen, and the believer who weighs the matters is the believer whose wisdom the Lord values, and the test is the teaching that the wisdom is the thing that the believer must use. The verse is the record of the test, and the teaching of the wisdom that the trial requires.",
  "42": "“So when she arrived, it was said ˹to her˺, “Is your throne like this?” She replied, “It looks to be the same. We have ˹already˺ received knowledge ˹of Solomon’s prophethood˺ before this ˹miracle˺, and have submitted ˹to Allah˺.”” records the arrival, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the arrival: or \"fa-lamma ja'at qeela a-hakatha 'arshuki qalat ka-annahu huwa wa ooteena al-'ilma min qabliha wa kunna muslimeen,\" and the commentators note the arrival, for the queen recognized the throne, and the arrival is the recognition, the acknowledgment of the truth, and the verse is the record of the arrival, the recognition that the following verse qualifies, and the commentators note the significance, for the recognition is the truth, and the verse is the record of the arrival, the recognition of the queen. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the arrival is the recognition and the acknowledgment is the truth, and the verse is the record of the arrival, the recognition that the following verse qualifies.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the arrival, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own certainty. The believer is taught that the recognition of the truth is the thing that the believer must make, and the one who recognizes is the one whose certainty the Lord values, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the arrival of the queen is the teaching, the declaration that the recognition is the thing that the believer must make, and the believer who reads the arrival is the believer whose certainty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the recognition of the truth: the queen recognized the throne, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the arrival is the teaching that the recognition is the thing that the believer must make. The verse is the record of the arrival, and the teaching of the certainty that the recognition produces.",
  "43": "“But she had been hindered by what she used to worship instead of Allah, for she was indeed from a disbelieving people.”” records the hindrance, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the hindrance: or \"wa saddaha ma kanat ta'budu min dooni Allahi innaha kanat min qawmin kafireen,\" and the commentators note the hindrance, for the queen was hindered by the worship, and the hindrance is the teaching, the declaration that the false worship blocks the truth, and the verse is the record of the hindrance, the teaching that the following verse resolves, and the commentators note the significance, for the hindrance is the falsehood, and the verse is the record of the hindrance, the teaching of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the hindrance is the falsehood and the block is the worship, and the verse is the record of the hindrance, the teaching that the following verse resolves.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the hindrance, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own certainty. The believer is taught that the false worship blocks the truth, and the one who is hindered is the one whose hindrance the truth removes, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the hindrance of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the falsehood is the thing that the truth removes, and the believer who reads the hindrance is the believer whose certainty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the block of the falsehood: the queen was hindered and freed, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the hindrance is the teaching that the falsehood is the thing that the truth removes. The verse is the record of the hindrance, and the teaching of the certainty that the truth produces.",
  "44": "“Then she was told, “Enter the palace.” But when she saw the hall, she thought it was a body of water, so she bared her legs. Solomon said. “It is just a palace paved with crystal.” ˹At last˺ she declared, “My Lord! I have certainly wronged my soul. Now I ˹fully˺ submit myself along with Solomon to Allah, the Lord of all worlds.”” records the submission, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the submission: or \"qeela laha udkhulee as-sarha fa-lamma ra'athu hasibathu lujjatan wa kashafat 'an saqayha qala innahu sarhun mumarradun min qawareera qalat rabbi innee zalamtu nafsee wa aslamtu ma'a Sulaymana lillahi rabbi al-'alameen,\" and the commentators note the submission, for the queen submitted, and the submission is the conversion, the declaration of the faith, and the verse is the record of the submission, the conversion that the account closes with, and the commentators note the significance, for the submission is the conversion, and the verse is the record of the submission, the conversion of the queen. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the submission is the conversion and the declaration is the faith, and the verse is the record of the submission, the conversion that the account closes with.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the submission, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own faith. The believer is taught that the submission is the conversion, and the one who submits is the one whose faith the Lord values, and the believer who declares the faith is the believer whose faith the Lord confirms, and the submission of the queen is the teaching, the declaration that the faith is the thing that the believer must declare, and the believer who reads the submission is the believer whose faith the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the conversion of the truth: the queen submitted and declared, and the believer who declares the faith is the believer whose faith the Lord values, and the submission is the teaching that the faith is the thing that the believer must declare. The verse is the record of the submission, and the teaching of the faith that the conversion establishes.",
  "45": "“And We certainly sent to the people of Thamûd their brother Ṣâliḥ, proclaiming, “Worship Allah,” but they suddenly split into two opposing groups.”” records the mission, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the mission: or \"wa laqad arsalna ila Thamooda akhahum Salihan ani u'budoo Allaha fa-itha hum fareeqani yakhtasimoon,\" and the commentators note the mission, for Salih called the worship, and the verse is the record of the mission, the call that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the split is the division, and the verse is the record of the mission, the call of Salih. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the mission is the call and the split is the division, and the verse is the record of the mission, the call that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the mission, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own worship. The believer is taught that the call of the worship is the message, and the one who worships is the one whose worship the Lord values, and the believer who calls to the truth is the believer whose calling the Lord accepts, and the mission of Salih is the teaching, the declaration that the worship is the thing that the believer must call to, and the believer who reads the mission is the believer whose worship the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the call of the worship: Salih called and the people split, and the believer who worships the One is the believer whose worship the Lord values, and the mission is the teaching that the worship is the thing that the believer must call to. The verse is the record of the mission, and the teaching of the worship that the call requires.",
  "46": "“He urged ˹the disbelieving group˺, “O my people! Why do you ˹seek to˺ hasten the torment rather than grace? If only you sought Allah’s forgiveness so you may be shown mercy!”” records the urging, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the urging: or \"qala ya qawmi lima tasta'jiloona bis-sayyi'ati qabla al-hasanati law la tastaghfiroona Allaha la'allakum turhamoon,\" and the commentators note the urging, for Salih urged the forgiveness, and the urging is the mercy, the call of the prophet, and the verse is the record of the urging, the mercy that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the urging is the mercy, and the verse is the record of the urging, the mercy of Salih. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the urging is the mercy and the forgiveness is the call, and the verse is the record of the urging, the mercy that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the urging, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own hope. The believer is taught that the seeking of the forgiveness is the mercy, and the one who urges is the one whose mercy the Lord values, and the believer who hopes the mercy is the believer whose hope the Lord fulfills, and the urging of Salih is the teaching, the declaration that the mercy is the thing that the believer must seek, and the believer who reads the urging is the believer whose hope the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the mercy of the urging: Salih urged the forgiveness, and the believer who hopes the mercy is the believer whose hope the Lord fulfills, and the urging is the teaching that the mercy is the thing that the believer must seek. The verse is the record of the urging, and the teaching of the hope that the mercy carries.",
  "47": "“They replied, “You and your followers are a bad omen for us.” He responded, “Your omens are destined by Allah. In fact, you are ˹only˺ a people being tested.”” records the reply, and the commentators note the two parts of the reply. The verse records the reply: or \"qaloo ittayyarna bika wa biman ma'aka qala ta'irukum 'inda Allahi bal antum qawmun tuftanoon,\" and the commentators note the reply, for the people blamed the omen, and the reply is the correction, the declaration that the omen is the Lord's, and the verse is the record of the reply, the correction that the following verse describes, and the commentators note the significance, for the correction is the truth, and the verse is the record of the reply, the correction of Salih. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the reply is the correction and the omen is the Lord's, and the verse is the record of the reply, the correction that the following verse describes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the reply, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own certainty. The believer is taught that the omen is the thing that the Lord decrees, and the one who corrects is the one whose certainty the Lord values, and the believer who trusts the decree is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the reply of Salih is the teaching, the declaration that the omen is the thing that the believer must attribute to the Lord, and the believer who reads the reply is the believer whose certainty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the attribution of the omen: Salih corrected the blame, and the believer who trusts the decree is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the reply is the teaching that the omen is the thing that the believer must attribute to the Lord. The verse is the record of the reply, and the teaching of the certainty that the correction produces.",
  "48": "“And there were in the city nine ˹elite˺ men who spread corruption in the land, never doing what is right.”” records the nine, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the nine: or \"wa kana fi al-madeenati tis'atu rahtin yufsidoona fi al-ardi wa la yuslihoon,\" and the commentators note the nine, for the nine corruptors are named, and the verse is the record of the nine, the description that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the corruption is the sin, and the verse is the record of the nine, the description of the corruptors. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the nine are the corruptors and the corruption is the sin, and the verse is the record of the nine, the description that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the nine, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own justice. The believer is taught that the corruption is the sin, and the one who corrupts is the one whose corruption the Lord weighs, and the believer who sets things right is the believer whose justice the Lord values, and the nine of the verse are the teaching, the declaration that the corruption is the thing that the believer must avoid, and the believer who reads the nine is the believer whose justice the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the danger of the corruption: the nine corruptors were destroyed, and the believer who sets things right is the believer whose justice the Lord values, and the nine are the teaching that the corruption is the thing that the believer must avoid. The verse is the record of the nine, and the teaching of the justice that the corruption requires.",
  "49": "“They vowed, “Let us swear by Allah that we will take him and his family down by night. Then we will certainly say to his ˹closest˺ heirs, ‘We did not witness the murder of his family. We are definitely telling the truth.’”” records the plot, and the commentators note the two parts of the plot. The verse records the plot: or \"taqasamoo billahi la-nubayyitannahu wa ahlahu thumma la-naqoolanna li-waliyyihi ma shahidna mahlika ahlihi wa inna la-sadiqoon,\" and the commentators note the plot, for the nine vowed the murder, and the plot is the scheme, the plan of the corruptors, and the verse is the record of the plot, the scheme that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the plot is the scheme, and the verse is the record of the plot, the scheme of the nine. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the plot is the scheme and the vow is the plan, and the verse is the record of the plot, the scheme that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the plot, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own trust. The believer is taught that the plot of the falsehood is the thing that the truth withstands, and the one who is plotted is the one whose trust the Lord values, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the plot of the nine is the teaching, the declaration that the scheme is the thing that the Lord frustrates, and the believer who reads the plot is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the scheme of the falsehood: the nine plotted and were destroyed, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the plot is the teaching that the scheme is the thing that the Lord frustrates. The verse is the record of the plot, and the teaching of the trust that the scheme requires.",
  "50": "“And ˹so˺ they made a plan, but We too made a plan, while they were unaware.”” records the counter-plan, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the counter-plan: or \"wa makaroo makran wa makarna makran wa hum la yash'uroon,\" and the commentators note the counter-plan, for the Lord planned against the nine, and the counter-plan is the judgment, the frustration of the scheme, and the verse is the record of the counter-plan, the judgment that the following verse reflects upon, and the commentators note the significance, for the counter-plan is the judgment, and the verse is the record of the counter-plan, the judgment of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the counter-plan is the judgment and the frustration is the end, and the verse is the record of the counter-plan, the judgment that the following verse reflects upon.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the counter-plan, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own trust. The believer is taught that the plan of the Lord is the thing that the believer trusts, and the one who trusts is the one whose trust the Lord confirms, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose trust the Lord values, and the counter-plan of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the judgment is the thing that the Lord brings, and the believer who reads the counter-plan is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the plan of the Lord: the nine plotted and the Lord planned, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the counter-plan is the teaching that the judgment is the thing that the Lord brings. The verse is the record of the counter-plan, and the teaching of the trust that the judgment produces.",
  "51": "“See then what the consequences of their plan were: We ˹utterly˺ destroyed them and their people all together.”” records the result, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the result: or \"fanzur kayfa kana 'aqibatu makrihim anna dammarnahum wa qawmahum ajma'een,\" and the commentators note the result, for the Lord destroyed the nine and the people, and the result is the judgment, the end of the corruptors, and the verse is the record of the result, the judgment that the following verse describes, and the commentators note the significance, for the destruction is the judgment, and the verse is the record of the result, the judgment of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the result is the judgment and the destruction is the end, and the verse is the record of the result, the judgment that the following verse describes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the result, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own certainty. The believer is taught that the destruction of the corruptors is the judgment, and the one who corrupts is the one whose end the Lord declares, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the result of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the corruption is the path of the ruin, and the believer who reads the result is the believer whose certainty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the end of the corruption: the nine were destroyed, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the result is the teaching that the corruption is the path of the ruin. The verse is the record of the result, and the teaching of the certainty that the judgment produces.",
  "52": "“So their homes are there, ˹but completely˺ ruined because of their wrongdoing. Surely in this is a lesson for people of knowledge.”” records the ruins, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the ruins: or \"fa-tilka buyootuhum khawiyatan bima zalamoo inna fee thalika la-ayatan li-qawmin ya'lamoon,\" and the commentators note the ruins, for the homes are the ruined, and the ruins are the witness, the lesson of the wrong, and the verse is the record of the ruins, the witness that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the ruins are the witness, and the verse is the record of the ruins, the witness of the destruction. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the ruins are the witness and the lesson is the knowledge, and the verse is the record of the ruins, the witness that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the ruins, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own reflection. The believer is taught that the ruins of the wrongdoers are the witness, and the one who reflects is the one whose reflection the Lord values, and the believer who reads the history is the believer whose reflection the Lord accepts, and the ruins of the verse are the teaching, the declaration that the reflection is the thing that the believer must do, and the believer who reads the ruins is the believer whose reflection the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the witness of the ruins: the homes are the ruined, and the believer who reflects is the believer whose reflection the Lord values, and the ruins are the teaching that the reflection is the thing that the believer must do. The verse is the record of the ruins, and the teaching of the reflection that the witness invites.",
  "53": "“And We delivered those who were faithful and were mindful ˹of Allah˺.”” records the deliverance, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the deliverance: or \"wa anjayna allatheena amanoo wa kanoo yattaqoon,\" and the commentators note the deliverance, for the Lord saved the faithful, and the deliverance is the mercy, the rescue of the believers, and the verse is the record of the deliverance, the mercy that the following verse opens the next story with, and the commentators note the significance, for the deliverance is the mercy, and the verse is the record of the deliverance, the rescue of the faithful. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the deliverance is the mercy and the rescue is the reward, and the verse is the record of the deliverance, the mercy that the following verse opens the next story with.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the deliverance, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own hope. The believer is taught that the deliverance of the Lord is the mercy, and the one who is saved is the one whose faith the Lord values, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose salvation the Lord grants, and the deliverance of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the rescue is the thing that the Lord grants, and the believer who reads the deliverance is the believer whose hope the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the mercy of the salvation: the faithful were delivered, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose salvation the Lord grants, and the deliverance is the teaching that the rescue is the thing that the Lord grants. The verse is the record of the deliverance, and the teaching of the hope that the mercy carries.",
  "54": "“And ˹remember˺ Lot, when he rebuked ˹the men of˺ his people, “Do you commit that shameful deed while you can see ˹one another˺?”” records the rebuke, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the rebuke: or \"wa Lootan ith qala li-qawmihi a-ta'toona al-fahishata wa antum tubsiroon,\" and the commentators note the rebuke, for Lot rebuked the sin, and the rebuke is the correction, the exposure of the shameful, and the verse is the record of the rebuke, the correction that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the rebuke is the correction, and the verse is the record of the rebuke, the correction of Lot. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the rebuke is the correction and the sin is the shameful, and the verse is the record of the rebuke, the correction that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the rebuke, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own purity. The believer is taught that the rebuke of the sin is the correction, and the one who rebukes is the one whose purity the Lord values, and the believer who guards the chastity is the believer whose purity the Lord accepts, and the rebuke of Lot is the teaching, the declaration that the purity is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the rebuke is the believer whose purity the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the correction of the sin: Lot rebuked the shameful, and the believer who guards the chastity is the believer whose purity the Lord values, and the rebuke is the teaching that the purity is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the rebuke, and the teaching of the purity that the chastity requires.",
  "55": "“Do you really lust after men instead of women? In fact, you are ˹only˺ a people acting ignorantly.”” completes the rebuke, and the commentators note the two parts of the completion. The verse completes the rebuke: or \"a-innakum la-ta'toona ar-rijala shahwatan min dooni an-nisa'i bal antum qawmun tajhaloon,\" and the commentators note the completion, for Lot rebuked the lust, and the completion is the correction, the exposure of the ignorance, and the verse is the record of the completion, the correction that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the ignorance is the sin, and the verse is the record of the completion, the correction of Lot. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the completion is the correction and the ignorance is the sin, and the verse is the record of the completion, the correction that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the completion, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own purity. The believer is taught that the corruption of the nature is the ignorance, and the one who rebukes is the one whose purity the Lord values, and the believer who guards the chastity is the believer whose purity the Lord accepts, and the completion of Lot is the teaching, the declaration that the purity is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the completion is the believer whose purity the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the correction of the ignorance: Lot rebuked the lust, and the believer who guards the chastity is the believer whose purity the Lord values, and the completion is the teaching that the purity is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the purity that the chastity requires.",
  "56": "“But his people’s only response was to say, “Expel Lot’s followers from your land! They are a people who wish to remain chaste!”” records the reply, and the commentators note the two parts of the reply. The verse records the reply: or \"fa-ma kana jawaba qawmihi illa an qaloo akhrijoo ala Lootin min qaryatikum innahum unasun yatatahharoon,\" and the commentators note the reply, for the people demanded the expulsion, and the reply is the rejection, the contempt of the purity, and the verse is the record of the reply, the rejection that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the contempt is the rejection, and the verse is the record of the reply, the rejection of the people. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the reply is the rejection and the contempt is the purity, and the verse is the record of the reply, the rejection that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the reply, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own patience. The believer is taught that the contempt of the purity is the rejection, and the one who is expelled is the one whose patience the Lord values, and the believer who bears the contempt is the believer whose patience the Lord accepts, and the reply of the people is the teaching, the declaration that the contempt is the thing that the falsehood uses, and the believer who reads the reply is the believer whose patience the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the patience of the contempt: Lot was expelled and saved, and the believer who bears the contempt is the believer whose patience the Lord values, and the reply is the teaching that the contempt is the thing that the falsehood uses. The verse is the record of the reply, and the teaching of the patience that the contempt requires.",
  "57": "“So We delivered him and his family, except his wife. We had destined her to be one of the doomed.”” records the deliverance, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the deliverance: or \"fa-anjaynahu wa ahlahu illa imra'atahu qaddarnaha mina al-ghabireen,\" and the commentators note the deliverance, for the Lord saved Lot and the family, and the deliverance is the mercy, the rescue of the faithful, and the verse is the record of the deliverance, the mercy that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the exception is the teaching, and the verse is the record of the deliverance, the rescue of Lot. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the deliverance is the mercy and the exception is the faith, and the verse is the record of the deliverance, the mercy that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the deliverance, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own hope. The believer is taught that the salvation of the Lord is the mercy, and the one who is saved is the one whose faith the Lord values, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose salvation the Lord grants, and the deliverance of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the faith is the condition of the salvation, and the believer who reads the deliverance is the believer whose hope the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the condition of the salvation: the wife was excepted, and the believer who holds the faith is the believer whose salvation the Lord grants, and the deliverance is the teaching that the faith is the condition. The verse is the record of the deliverance, and the teaching of the hope that the mercy carries.",
  "58": "“And We poured upon them a rain ˹of brimstone˺. How evil was the rain of those who had been warned!”” records the punishment, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the punishment: or \"wa amtarna 'alayhim mataran fa-sa'a mataru al-munthareen,\" and the commentators note the punishment, for the Lord rained the brimstone, and the punishment is the judgment, the end of the people, and the verse is the record of the punishment, the judgment that the following verse reflects upon, and the commentators note the significance, for the rain is the judgment, and the verse is the record of the punishment, the judgment of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the punishment is the judgment and the rain is the end, and the verse is the record of the punishment, the judgment that the following verse reflects upon.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the punishment, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own fear. The believer is taught that the punishment of the warned is the judgment, and the one who is warned is the one whose end the Lord declares, and the believer who fears the judgment is the believer whose fear the Lord values, and the punishment of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the judgment is the thing that the Lord brings, and the believer who reads the punishment is the believer whose fear the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the judgment of the Lord: the rain fell upon the warned, and the believer who fears the judgment is the believer whose fear the Lord values, and the punishment is the teaching that the judgment is the thing that the Lord brings. The verse is the record of the punishment, and the teaching of the fear that the judgment produces.",
  "59": "\"Say, O Prophet, 'Praise be to Allah, and peace be upon the servants He has chosen.'\" After the long chain of stories, the Prophet Muhammad is told to speak praise and peace. Praise, \"al-hamd,\" belongs to Allah for His signs, His rescue of believers, and His justice against corruptors. Peace is sent upon the chosen servants, the prophets and the faithful just named: Moses, David, Solomon, Salih, Lot, and those delivered with them.\n\nThe chosen, \"istafa,\" are selected by Allah for message and for mercy. Greeting them with peace is a refusal of the Meccan habit of mocking messengers. If Allah has chosen them, the right speech is \"salam,\" not omen-talk and not expulsion.\n\nThen the verse turns: \"Ask the disbelievers, 'Which is better: Allah or whatever gods they associate with Him?'\" The question is almost too plain. After gardens of proof in the stories, can a carved partner or a worshipped sun stand beside the Lord who spoke to Moses, taught Solomon, saved Salih's faithful, and rained on Lot's town?\n\nThe Arabic challenge is \"a-Allah khayrun amma yushrikun,\" is Allah better or what they associate? The answer is built into the asking. No honest scale remains. Yet the next verses will not stop at the question. They will list creation, rain, gardens, the stable earth, rivers, mountains, the barrier between waters, the answer to the distressed, guidance in darkness, winds, origin and resurrection, and provision. The question of this verse is the heading of that whole argument.\n\nA listener in Mecca who loved ancestors' idols is being asked to compare, not merely to inherit. Comparison is a mercy. It invites the mind. The stories showed what happens when people refuse the comparison. The praise and peace at the start keep the Prophet's own heart in gratitude while he asks a question that may be met with stubborn silence.",
  "60": "\"Or ask them, 'Who created the heavens and the earth'\" begins the first detailed challenge after the general question of which is better. Creation of the heavens and the earth is the widest work. No idol of Mecca, and no sun of Sheba, laid out the sky and the ground.\n\nThe question continues, \"and sends down rain for you from the sky, by which We cause delightful gardens to grow?\" Rain is not a vague blessing. It is \"for you.\" Gardens, \"hada'iq dhata bahjah,\" are gardens with brightness and joy. The speaker shifts to \"We cause,\" so that the listener hears Allah claiming the growth, not a nature that runs alone.\n\n\"You could never cause their trees to grow\" cuts human boast. A farmer plants and waters, but he cannot create the life of the tree. The sentence is humble and exact. Human skill is real. Human creating of the tree's being is not.\n\n\"Was it another god besides Allah?\" returns the refrain that will mark this whole passage. The expected answer is no. \"Absolutely not! But they are a people who set up equals to Allah!\" The word \"ya'dilun\" means they make others equal, they set up rivals, they turn aside to counterparts. They know, in the way desert people know rain, that idols do not grow trees. Still they set up equals.\n\nThe rain of brimstone in Lot's story and this rain of gardens stand near each other on purpose. The same Lord sends both. To set up equals is to insult both the mercy of gardens and the justice of warning. The next verse will leave the garden and speak of the earth's stability, rivers, mountains, and the barrier between waters, more works that no partner performed.\n\n'For you' also aims the verse at the very people being argued with. The rain feeds their caravans' grass, their markets' grain, their families' bread, and the question makes the listeners the recipients of the evidence against their idols. An argument they could wave away as abstract becomes a matter of yesterday's supper, and supper is harder to deny than a creed.",
  "61": "\"Or ask them, 'Who made the earth a place of settlement'\" moves from sky and gardens to the ground under the feet. A \"qarar,\" a place of settlement, is earth steady enough to live on, not a lurching ruin. Human cities, including Mecca, depend on that gift before they depend on any idol.\n\n\"Caused rivers to flow through it\" adds movement of fresh water across that settled ground. \"Placed firm mountains upon it\" adds the weights and pegs that the Quran often ties to the earth's stillness. \"And set a barrier between fresh and salt bodies of water\" points to the strange meeting of seas or of river and sea, where waters remain distinct by a barrier, \"hajiz,\" that humans did not build.\n\nEach gift is a question. Who did this? \"Was it another god besides Allah?\" The refrain returns. \"Absolutely not! But most of them do not know.\" The close is different from the previous verse. There they set up equals. Here most of them lack knowledge. Both can be true. Some stubbornly equalize. Many simply do not think.\n\nThe barrier between waters is a sign that later generations have also found striking. The verse does not ask for a modern ocean lecture. It asks for honesty. The things that make settled life possible are not the work of the partners.\n\nSolomon's crystal palace and this firm earth are different kinds of making. One is a gift through a prophet's kingdom. The other is the common floor of all people. The argument is that even the common floor is already enough to end \"shirk,\" the associating of others with Allah. The next question will leave landscape and enter the human cry of distress, a proof from the heart rather than from mountains.\n\n'Most of them do not know' also grades the audience more gently than the verse before it. There, active equalizers; here, the unthinking many. The distinction matters for the Prophet's approach: some opponents need confrontation, and many need only to be helped to notice what they walk on. A religion that treats every ignorance as malice will talk past half the people it addresses.",
  "62": "\"Or ask them, 'Who responds to the distressed when they cry to Him'\" turns from mountains to the breaking voice. The \"mudtarr\" is the person in desperate need, cornered, out of tricks. When that person cries, who answers? The verse assumes a fact the pagan Arabs themselves knew in storms and illness: they would call on Allah. Other verses say that when harm touches people at sea they remember Him, then they forget when they land.\n\n\"Relieving their affliction\" is the second act. Response is not only hearing. It is the lifting of the \"su',\" the harm. Then a third act: \"and Who makes you successors in the earth.\" Humans inherit the land generation after generation, \"khulafa.\" Power to dwell and replace those before you is not self-made.\n\n\"Is it another god besides Allah?\" The refrain comes again, now in a slightly different English dress. \"Yet you are hardly mindful!\" The charge is lack of \"tadhakkur,\" reminder. They know the cry of distress in their bones and still will not keep that knowledge awake.\n\nThis verse matters because a person might admit Allah created the heavens and still take daily fears to a charm, a saint, a star, or an idol. The Quran says the One who answers the desperate is Allah. To call others in that moment as if they shared His power is the very association under attack.\n\nThe queen of Sheba was not in a sinking ship, yet she was distressed by a letter and a coming force. Her final cry was, \"My Lord! I have certainly wronged my soul.\" That is the mudtarr finding the right address. The verse asks Mecca to do the same before a rain of stones or a last Hour makes the cry too late. The next sign will be guidance in the dark of land and sea, and winds that bring mercy.",
  "63": "\"Or ask them, 'Who guides you in the darkness of the land and sea'\" speaks to a people who traveled caravans and ships. Darkness here is the dark of night and the dark of being lost, when stars, landmarks, and coasts become the difference between life and death. Who sets those signs and brings the traveler through?\n\n\"And sends the winds ushering in His mercy\" adds the winds that go ahead of rain. Mercy in this picture is often the rainfall itself, announced by moving air. Sailors and herders both knew that a wind could mean relief. The verse gives that relief a Sender.\n\n\"Is it another god besides Allah?\" The question does not grow tired. Each new gift needs the same honesty. \"Exalted is Allah above what they associate with Him!\" This close is praise, \"ta'ala Allah,\" high is Allah above the partners. The partners are not merely weaker. They are beneath His height.\n\nGuidance in darkness pairs with the chapter's opening claim that the Book is a guide for believers. Physical guidance through night and spiritual guidance through revelation come from one Lord. To take a star as a god because it helps in the dark is to confuse the sign with the One who hung the sign.\n\nThe winds \"ushering in His mercy\" also stand opposite the rain of brimstone. Winds can go before a garden or, in other stories, before a punishing storm. They are not luck. They are sent. The next verse will rise from travel and weather to the origin of creation, its resurrection, and daily provision, then demand proof from those who still claim partners.\n\nThe pairing of darkness and wind also matches the two halves of a caravan's fear: being lost, and being stranded. Guidance answers the first, the herald winds of rain answer the second, and the verse hands both anxieties to one Sender. The listener who has felt either fear recognises the argument immediately, which is why the proof is drawn from travel rather than from theory.",
  "64": "\"Or ask them, 'Who originates the creation then resurrects it'\" brings the argument to the two ends of existence. Originating, \"yabda'u al-khalq,\" is the first making. Resurrecting, \"yu'iduhu,\" is the bringing back. The pagan listener often admitted the first and denied the second. The verse ties them in one breath. The One who started is the One who returns.\n\n\"And gives you provisions from the heavens and the earth\" keeps the matter from becoming only a debate about the far future. Provision, \"rizq,\" is today's food, rain, crops, and what the soil yields. The God of resurrection is the God of supper.\n\n\"Is it another god besides Allah?\" One last time in this series the question falls. Then a new command: \"Say, O Prophet, 'Show me your proof, if what you say is true.'\" The partners now owe \"burhan,\" a clear proof. Stories, heavens, rain, gardens, earth, rivers, mountains, barriers, answers to distress, guidance, winds, origin, return, and provision have been presented. What proof do the associates have?\n\nThe demand for proof is a kindness to the mind. The Quran does not ask for blind insult of ancestors. It asks for evidence. If none exists, the honest act is to drop the partners.\n\nMeccans said resurrection was an ancient fable, as the next group of verses will quote. This verse has already answered them in principle: the Originator can resurrect, and the Provider is the same Lord. The next verse will close another door, the claim that someone besides Allah knows the unseen, including the hour of rising.\n\n'Show me your proof' also sets a rule that outlives the scene. The demand for evidence runs one way through this passage: creation is presented as the believers' proof, and the associates are asked for theirs. Ever since, the Quran's method has invited the same test, and the honest answer it predicts is silence, because a partner that cannot grow a tree cannot produce a witness either.",
  "65": "\"Say, O Prophet, 'None in the heavens and the earth has knowledge of the unseen except Allah.'\" After demanding proof for partners, the Quran shuts the claim of hidden knowledge. The unseen, \"al-ghayb,\" is what lies beyond created sight: the future, the Hour, the secrets of the womb of time. Angels, jinn, humans, and the beings of the heavens do not own it. Allah alone knows it.\n\nSolomon's story in this chapter could be misread. A hoopoe brought news. A man of Scripture brought a throne in a blink. Jinn spoke of strength. None of that means creatures possess the unseen as Allah does. They know what they are taught or what they observe. This verse protects tawhid, the oneness of Allah, in the field of knowledge.\n\n\"Nor do they know when they will be resurrected.\" The most urgent unseen for the deniers is the time of rising. They ask \"when\" as if the messenger were hiding a date, or as if not knowing a date made the event false. The verse says no one in the heavens or the earth knows that when. Not knowing the hour is not ignorance of Allah. It is the created condition.\n\nA common mistake is to go to fortune-tellers, star-readers, or anyone who claims the ghayb. This verse leaves no room for that trade. Another mistake is to think the Prophet failed because he did not give a calendar of the Hour. He is commanded to say that none knows except Allah.\n\nThe next verse will describe the deniers' so-called knowledge of the Hereafter as ignorance, doubt, and blindness. This verse has prepared that charge. If they do not know the unseen, their confident mockery of resurrection is empty. They speak without the only knowledge that would settle the \"when.\" \n\n'Nor do they know when they will be resurrected' also removes a lever the mockers thought they held. Their strongest card was the messenger's lack of a date, and the verse simply concedes the point and re-sits it: no creature holds that card, so its absence proves nothing against the message. An argument built on a gap in created knowledge collapses once the gap is acknowledged as designed.",
  "66": "\"No! Their knowledge of the Hereafter amounts to ignorance.\" The English line captures a dense Arabic turn: their knowledge about the last day has not matured; it has collapsed into not-knowing. They talk as if they have researched the matter and found it false. The Quran says the result of their \"knowledge\" is ignorance.\n\n\"In fact, they are in doubt about it.\" The first step down is ignorance. The second is \"shakk,\" doubt. They are not calmly sure there is no rising. They waver, yet they mock, which is a strange combination. Mockery often hides a shaken heart.\n\n\"In truth, they are totally blind to it.\" The third step is \"amun,\" blindness. Doubt that is not treated becomes a refusal to see. The Hereafter is not dim because it lacks signs. It is dim because they have closed the eye that should use the signs just listed: origin and return, rain and ruin, delivered believers and empty houses.\n\nThe verse uses a climbing correction: no, in fact, in truth. Each clause is stronger. A teacher might stop at \"they do not know.\" The Quran says they do not know, they doubt, they are blind. That is why argument alone sometimes fails, as later verses will tell the Prophet he cannot make the dead hear.\n\nThis diagnosis also guards believers from envy of the deniers' confidence. That confidence is not knowledge. It is blindness wearing the mask of sophistication. The next verses will quote their actual sentences about dust and ancient fables, so the listener can hear the blindness in their own words.\n\nThe three-step descent also describes how the same soul can hold doubt and mockery at once. Ignorance is passive, doubt is unsettled, blindness is chosen; the mocker has travelled the full distance and speaks from the end of it. Believers meet the resulting confidence and mistake it for strength, when the verse has already diagnosed it as the final stage of not-seeing.",
  "67": "\"The disbelievers ask, 'When we and our fathers are reduced to dust, will we really be brought forth alive?'\" Now the blindness speaks. The question is built on a picture: bodies become dust, fathers already dust, so how can bringing forth happen? The appeal is to what the eye sees in graves.\n\nThe wording \"we and our fathers\" tries to make the case heavier. Not only we will be powder. The old generations are already powder. If anyone were coming back, the fathers would have come. The argument sounds practical. It treats decay as a disproof of power.\n\nThe Quran has just said that none knows the unseen except Allah, and that the Originator can resurrect. This question does not answer those points. It only stares at dust. Dust is real. The limitation of dust over Allah is not real.\n\nSimilar questions appear in many chapters. The repetition means the doubt was a standard line in Mecca, not a rare thought. \"Brought forth alive\" is said with disbelief, not with hope. The next verse will add that they have heard this promise before and have filed it under ancient fables.\n\nLot's people and Thamud also refused a world beyond their custom. Dust-talk is another custom of refusal. The body they cannot imagine being remade is the same body that eats provision from the heavens and the earth, provision they cannot create. The question would be honest if it ended in humility: how will Allah do this? It is not honest when it ends in a sneer. The following verse makes the sneer plain.\n\n'Reduced to dust' also borrows its force from a half-truth, which is why the question survives generations. Bodies really do decay; the error is the hidden premise that decay limits the Maker. The Quran's replies never dispute the dust; they dispute the premise, and the same reasoning applies wherever a true observation is made to carry a false conclusion.",
  "68": "\"We have already been promised this, as well as our forefathers earlier.\" The disbelievers claim boredom. The promise of rising is not new to them. Their fathers heard it. They hear it. Nothing has happened yet, so they treat delay as cancellation.\n\n\"This is nothing but ancient fables!\" is the stamp they put on revelation. \"Asatir al-awwalin,\" tales of the ancients, was a stock insult in Mecca against the Quran. They filed resurrection with old stories told to children, not with truth that binds adults.\n\nThe insult tries to reverse honor. What is old can be venerable, like the message of Noah and Abraham. They use oldness as a slur. Because Salih and Lot and Moses already warned, the Meccans say the warning is a recycled myth. The chapter's stories were meant to show the opposite: the warning is old because the truth is one.\n\nDelay of the Hour was explained in the verse that none knows when they will be resurrected. They turn that hidden timing into proof that the promise is a fable. Hidden timing is a test. They fail it by mockery.\n\nThe next command will not be a long philosophy of dust. It will be, travel the land and see the fate of the wicked. Empty homes of Thamud and overturned towns of Lot are not fables in the sand. They are remains. If the old warnings were only stories, why do the ruins match them? The Quran sends the mocker from the word \"fable\" to the road.\n\n'As well as our forefathers earlier' also reveals how tradition cuts both ways in their hands. Ancestry is cited as authority against the promise, though the promise is older than the ancestry: Noah warned, and so did those after him. What the mockers inherit is not the original hearing of the message but the accumulated habit of ignoring it, and they mistake the pile of years for evidence.",
  "69": "\"Say, O Prophet, 'Travel throughout the land and see the fate of the wicked.'\" The reply to \"ancient fables\" is a journey. \"Siru fi al-ard,\" travel in the earth. Look. The fate, \"aqibah,\" of the criminals is not only written. It is stamped on places.\n\n\"The wicked\" here are \"al-mujrimin,\" those who cut themselves off by crime and denial. The chapter has already walked the listener past Pharaoh's end, Thamud's ruined homes, and the evil rain of Lot's people. The Prophet is to send his people to such traces, or to any land where a similar end can still be read.\n\nTravel in the Quran is not always trade. Sometimes it is a school. A person who will not accept unseen resurrection may still accept a seen ruin. The ruin does not itself raise the dead. It shows that Allah's warning in this world is not a fable. The God who did that can do the bringing forth they mock.\n\n\"See\" repeats the earlier \"see then what the consequences of their plan were.\" The same eye is invited again. Knowledge in this chapter is often the knowledge that looks.\n\nThe verse does not tell the Prophet to invent new threats. It tells him to point at the earth. That is a calm answer to sarcasm. The following verse will care for the Prophet's own heart: do not grieve for them, and do not be distressed by their schemes. After commanding a public reply, Allah will command an inner rest. The travel is for the mockers. The comfort is for the messenger who has to listen to them call truth a fable.\n\n'Travel' also dignifies the physical act of going and looking. The Quran does not ask the skeptic to feel something or to trust the messenger's face; it asks him to walk to a site and read it. Faith in this Book is repeatedly invited through eyes, feet, and ruins, which leaves the stay-at-home mocker without even the excuse of distance.",
  "70": "\"Do not grieve for them\" is spoken to the Prophet after he is told to send people traveling to see the fate of the wicked. The command turns from the public answer to the messenger's heart. Grief here is the sorrow of a man who wants his people saved and watches them choose mockery instead. Other verses tell him not to nearly destroy himself with grief over their denial. This line belongs to that gentle series.\n\n\"Nor be distressed by their schemes\" names the second weight on him. They do not only disbelieve. They plot, as Thamud's nine plotted, as Mecca plotted against the Prophet. Distress, tightness in the chest, is a natural human response to schemes. Allah forbids that tightness from ruling him. Their \"makr\" is seen. It is not allowed to shrink his mission.\n\nThe two halves treat two different pains. Grief looks backward and inward at their loss. Distress looks at their active harm. Both are closed as masters of the Prophet's soul. He may feel, but he may not sink.\n\nThis is not coldness. A prophet who did not care would not need the command. The command exists because he cares too much for his own peace. Allah takes the burden of results. The Prophet carries the message. The travel-command of the previous verse is for them. This rest-command is for him.\n\nSchemes in this chapter have already been answered in the story of Salih: they made a plan, but We too made a plan, while they were unaware. The Prophet is to live that truth, not only recite it. When the next verse quotes them asking when the threat will come, he will answer without the panic of a man undone by grief. Inner rest is part of clear speech.\n\nThe pairing also matches the Prophet's two job hazards exactly: sorrow for those who will not be saved, and fear of those who scheme. One is answered with comfort, the other with the reminder that scheming was already answered in this chapter, against the nine. A heart kept free of both is not a heart without love or caution; it is a heart whose love and caution have both been handed their proper size.",
  "71": "\"They ask the believers, 'When will this threat come to pass, if what you say is true?'\" The mockery now takes the form of a calendar question. \"This threat\" is the promised punishment or the Hour they were told to fear. They do not ask in order to prepare. They ask in order to imply that delay means falsehood.\n\n\"If what you say is true\" puts the believers in the dock. The burden of proof, in their mouths, is a date. The previous verses already said that none in the heavens or the earth knows the unseen except Allah, and that they do not know when they will be resurrected. This question pretends that a true prophet must still produce a schedule.\n\nThe same spirit appeared when Salih asked why they hastened the torment rather than grace. Haste for punishment is not piety. It is a dare. Mecca dares the warning the way Thamud dared Salih.\n\nThe believers are the ones being asked, not only the Prophet. Everyday companions faced this taunt in markets and gatherings. The next verse will give the Prophet a sentence to say, so the community is not left fumbling for a clever date they were never given.\n\nA misconception is to treat every \"when\" as honest curiosity. Honest curiosity seeks how to be ready. This \"when\" seeks how to laugh. The wording \"if what you say is true\" already leans toward the charge of lying. The Quran will not satisfy that lean with a number. It will answer with nearness as a possibility, with bounty they already waste, and with the Lord's knowledge of what they hide.\n\n'They ask the believers' also shows the taunt working at street level. The question was not reserved for formal debates with the Prophet; ordinary Muslims met it in the market, and the verse records the plural askers and the plural asked. Scripture here acknowledges the social pressure of being laughed at, and the reply it supplies is one any companion could carry and reuse.",
  "72": "\"Say, O Prophet, 'Perhaps some of what you seek to hasten is close at hand.'\" The answer refuses a date and refuses a shrug. \"Perhaps\" keeps the unseen with Allah. \"Close at hand\" removes the comfort of thinking the threat is always far. Some of what they hurry toward may already be near: a worldly blow, a defeat, a death, or the first shadows of the Hour. The verse does not specify which piece. Nearness is enough to end careless delay.\n\nThey \"seek to hasten.\" The verb puts the haste on them, as Salih put it on his people. The Prophet does not invent a new punishment to match their dare. He warns that the very thing they drag forward may be at the door.\n\nEarly Muslims later saw parts of this nearness in the struggles of the prophetic years, but the wording stays broader than one battle. Any listener who asks \"when\" as a joke is told that joking does not push the matter into a safe century.\n\nThe sentence is short so it can be remembered and repeated. It matches the command not to grieve. A grieving messenger might over-speak, or fall silent. This line is measured. It neither names a day nor lets them sleep.\n\n\"Some of what you seek\" also hints that not all promised things arrive at once. Resurrection of all dust is one thing. A nearer taste of consequence may be another. The mocker who only watches the sky for the final blast may miss the nearer justice already walking. The next verse will explain why they are given time at all: the Lord is bountiful, and most of them meet that bounty with ingratitude.\n\n'Perhaps' also teaches the Prophet a rhetoric of restraint. He is not licensed to threaten a specific day, and he is not permitted to dismiss the danger either; the word chosen leaves the timetable with Allah while unsettling the mocker's ease. Believers have long noted that this single sentence models honest speech under provocation: definite about the reality, indefinite about the date, serious about both.",
  "73": "\"Surely your Lord is ever Bountiful to humanity\" explains the delay they misuse. The Lord of the Prophet is \"dhu fadl,\" possessor of bounty, toward people as people, not only toward believers. Rain, night for rest, day for sight, provision, and the holding back of sudden ruin are all fadl. If every dare were answered at once with stones, the world would not be a place of trial.\n\n\"But most of them are ungrateful.\" The word \"la yashkurun\" returns the theme Solomon spoke beside the throne: whoever is grateful, it is for his own good; whoever is ungrateful, the Lord is Self-Sufficient, Most Generous. Most people eat the bounty and do not thank. Delay, which is itself a bounty, becomes fuel for the question \"when.\"\n\nThe verse balances two facts that cynics separate. They say, if God were real and angry, He would strike now. The Quran says, He is real and bountiful, and the strike's delay is part of that bounty, and most waste it. Ingratitude is not only failing to say thanks. It is using extra time to mock the warning.\n\n\"Your Lord\" comforts the Prophet. The Lord who told him not to grieve is the same Lord whose bounty fills the mockers' markets. Their safety today is not proof against him. It is proof of Allah's fadl.\n\nSolomon asked to be inspired to thanks. This verse says most humans take the opposite road. The chapter keeps setting gratitude and ingratitude opposite each other, from the ant's valley to this comment on humanity. The next verse will add that the Lord knows what they hide and what they show, so the ungrateful heart is not a secret.\n\n'To humanity' also keeps the bounty unearned and general, and the point is structural: rain does not check beliefs before falling. A mercy gated by gratitude would not be mercy but wages, and the Quran insists on the difference. The question the mockers should be asking is not why punishment waits, but why breakfast arrived again this morning to people who used yesterday's to sneer.",
  "74": "\"And surely your Lord knows what their hearts conceal and what they reveal.\" Bounty might look, to a shallow eye, like absence of knowledge, as if Allah did not see the hidden sneer. This verse closes that thought. The Lord knows the concealed, \"ma tukinnu suduruhum,\" what the chests fold away, and He knows the revealed, what they announce.\n\nThe pair matches an earlier line in the hoopoe's speech: Allah knows what you conceal and what you reveal. The same Lord of the Mighty Throne is the Lord of this verse. Knowledge of secrets is not a new claim. It is applied now to the mockers of the Hour.\n\nConcealed things include doubt they will not admit, envy, plots, and the part of the heart that fears the warning while the mouth laughs. Revealed things include the question \"when,\" the charge of ancient fables, and the schemes that distress the Prophet. Both layers are seen.\n\nThis knowledge makes delay more serious, not less. If Allah did not know the hidden, delay might be neglect. Because He knows, delay is a known test. Ingratitude is recorded from the inside out.\n\nFor the Prophet, the verse is also relief. He does not need to uncover every chest. He cannot. The Lord who forbade his grief already reads those chests. The next verse widens the same truth from their hearts to every hidden thing in heaven and earth, written in a perfect Record. Personal knowledge becomes cosmic record-keeping, so no one imagines that only loud sins are filed.\n\nConcealment, in the Quran's picture, is also never complete even from the person concealing. A mocker can hide the fear from his friends and partly from himself, and the verse calmly states that a third Reader was never blocked. That is why knowledge of hearts, far from being a threat only, can be a relief: someone always knew the whole of it, including the parts that never got spoken.",
  "75": "\"For there is nothing hidden in the heavens or the earth without being written in a perfect Record.\" The reason the Lord knows chests is larger than those chests. No hidden thing, \"gha'ibah,\" in sky or ground escapes writing. The Record, \"kitab mubin,\" is a clear, perfect book. The chapter began by calling the Quran a clear Book. Here another \"clear book\" is the register of all that is concealed in creation.\n\nCommentators have understood this as the Preserved Tablet, or as Allah's complete knowledge spoken of as writing so that humans grasp that nothing is forgotten. The point for the mocker is simple. The unseen they deny is already written. The date they demand is known to Allah even if it is unknown to them. The scheme they whisper is not off the page.\n\n\"In the heavens or the earth\" leaves no third place to hide. Jinn in the lower heaven, secrets in houses, plots in a city of nine men, the queen's old sun-worship, the wife's inner loyalty to Lot's people—all sit inside this claim.\n\nA person might fear that such a Record is only a threat. It is also justice. Good that no one clapped for is written. Hidden patience is written. The next verses will praise the Quran as a clarifier and a mercy. The Record of hidden things and the Quran that is recited work together: one preserves reality, the other explains guidance. Neither is a fable of the ancients. Both are \"mubin,\" clear.\n\nThis verse also answers fortune-tellers from another side. They do not have the Record. Claiming the unseen is claiming what is only with Allah and His writing. The Prophet is told to say none knows the ghayb except Allah. This Record is why that sentence is true.\n\n'Written' also settles the matter of memory. Human records lose files, and human witnesses forget; writing in the Quran's idiom is the guarantee that nothing true is ever mislaid. The mockers' hidden words, the believers' hidden patience, the queen's hidden deliberation, all are kept with a keeper's precision, and the Day of Judgment is run from records that cannot decay.",
  "76": "\"Indeed, this Quran clarifies for the Children of Israel most of what they differ over.\" The Book that was called a guide at the opening is now given a special work among the Jews of the Prophet's time and their disputes. They differed over many matters of law, stories of prophets, and claims about Jesus and about their own scriptures. The Quran comes as a judge and explainer of most of those differences, \"yaqussu,\" it relates and makes clear.\n\n\"Most\" is an honest word. Not every minor historical quarrel is the Quran's subject. The great differences that affect faith and practice are brought into the light. Stories in this very chapter, Moses at the fire, David and Solomon, are part of that clarifying. Israel knew those names. The Quran retells them without the clutter of later argument, and with a single message: worship Allah.\n\nThis is not said to insult the Children of Israel. It is said to honor the Quran's role and to invite them. A book that settles differences is a mercy if people accept the settlement. If they do not, the next verses will say the Lord will judge among them.\n\nMeccan pagans called the Quran ancient fables. This verse says it is a clarification of live disputes among a people of earlier scripture. Fables do not settle scholarly and religious differences. A clear Book does.\n\nThe hoopoe said he came from Sheba with sure news. The Quran comes to Israel with sure news about what they contest. Sure news demands a response like Solomon's: we will see whether you are telling the truth, then a fair test, not a sneer. The following verse will name the Quran again as guide and mercy, but this time for the believers who take it as such.\n\n'Most' also protects the Book from being reduced to a dispute-settler. The Quran's main work is guidance and mercy, and it clarifies controversies as they serve that work, not as a catalogue of every quarrel a scholar might raise. The restraint is a signature: revelation answers what matters for souls, and leaves the rest to the fields it never claimed.",
  "77": "\"And it is truly a guide and mercy for the believers.\" The opening of the chapter said the Book is a guide and good news for the believers who pray, give, and are sure of the Hereafter. This later verse returns to that description with \"mercy\" in place of \"good news,\" or rather as its sister. Guidance shows the road. Mercy is the kindness of being shown it and of being saved by walking it.\n\n\"For the believers\" is the same limit as before. The Quran is itself guidance, but it becomes a lived guide and mercy for those who believe. Others can hear the same recitation and call it a fable. The difference is not that the Book changed. The receiving heart changed the result.\n\nAfter saying the Quran clarifies differences for the Children of Israel, this verse states the inner fruit for anyone who believes, Israelite or not. Clarification can remain an outside argument. Mercy enters the life.\n\nThe stories of this chapter are part of that mercy. A believer learns how to thank like Solomon, how to consult like the queen at her best, how not to plot like the nine, how not to expel the chaste like Lot's people, how to ask who created the gardens. Mercy is practical. It is not only a soft feeling.\n\nThe next verse will speak of the Lord judging between them with justice. Guide and mercy do not cancel judgment. They make the people who refuse the guide without excuse, and they make the people who accept it hopeful. The pair of verses, clarification and mercy, then judgment, is a complete picture of what a revealed Book does in the world.\n\n'For the believers' also makes the chapter's own audience the exhibit. Everything the surah has narrated, from Moses' fire to the ant's valley, functions as guidance precisely where faith receives it, and the same recitation passing an unbelieving ear settles nothing there. The Book does not adjust itself; hearts do, and the verse quietly makes reception the hinge of the whole difference.",
  "78": "\"Your Lord will certainly judge between them by His justice.\" The differences of the Children of Israel, and the disputes of all who argue over the Quran, do not hang forever. The Lord will judge, \"yaqdi,\" with His judgment. \"By His justice\" means the verdict is not tribal and not random. \"Hukm\" here is wise ruling.\n\n\"For He is the Almighty, All-Knowing.\" The two names \"al-Aziz\" and \"al-Alim\" guarantee the judgment. Might means no party can block the sentence. Knowledge means no file is missing. Together they answer the fear that history is only the victory of the loud.\n\nMoses heard \"the Almighty, All-Wise\" at the fire. Here the pair is Almighty and All-Knowing. Wisdom and knowledge both sit with might. The names fit the task of judging differences. A weak judge cannot enforce. An ignorant judge cannot be fair.\n\n\"Between them\" can include Israel, the believers, and the pagans. All the files of this chapter wait for that court: Pharaoh who knew the signs and denied, the queen who submitted, Thamud who planned, Lot's people who expelled, the mockers who asked when.\n\nThe Prophet is not told to finish every argument in the street. He is told that the Lord will judge. That sentence works with \"do not grieve\" and with \"put your trust\" in the next verse. Human debate has a limit. Divine justice does not. The Quran clarifies most of what they differ over. What people still refuse will not remain an open tie. The Almighty, All-Knowing will close it.\n\n'Between them' also spares the Prophet a role he was never given. Judge among scripture's disputants is above his pay and above his lifespan; messenger is his office, and the verse hands the verdict upward. Every believer who argues religion has the same boundary to learn: present, clarify, invite, and then leave the closing of the case to the Almighty, All-Knowing.",
  "79": "\"So put your trust in Allah\" is the practical fruit of knowing that the Lord will judge with might and knowledge. Trust, \"tawakkul,\" is to place the weight of the affair on Allah while still doing the work of reciting and warning. It is not sitting down. It is refusing to let results become a second god.\n\n\"For you are surely upon the Path of clear truth.\" The reason he can trust is not a guess about tomorrow. It is a fact about today. He stands on \"al-haqq al-mubin,\" the clear truth. The chapter's first verse called the Book \"mubin.\" The Prophet's path is that same clarity. He does not need the crowd's agreement to know where he stands.\n\nThis address is intimate. \"You\" is the messenger whose grief and distress were forbidden, whose mouth was given answers about nearness and proof and the unseen. Now his inner act is named: trust. The path is already clear. Trust keeps him on it when schemes and taunts try to pull him into their weather.\n\nCompanions learned tawakkul from this kind of verse as well as from the Prophet's life in poverty, travel, and battle. They worked, then left the harvest to Allah. A misconception is that trust means no planning. Solomon planned a letter, a test of the throne, a disguised seat. He also said, this is by the grace of my Lord to test me. That mix is tawakkul.\n\nThe next two verses will limit the Prophet from the other side. He cannot make the dead hear. Trust does not mean he will convert everyone. Trust means he remains on the clear truth whether they hear or turn their backs.\n\n'Clear truth' also anchors trust in something checkable rather than in mood. The Prophet is not told to trust because things will go well; he is told to trust because he stands on truth that can be seen to be clear, in a Book whose arguments this chapter has run from gardens to ruins. Trust with that foundation survives bad weeks, which is precisely what it is for.",
  "80": "\"You certainly cannot make the dead hear the truth.\" The dead here are first a picture of those whose hearts have died, though the wording also reminds of literal corpses. The Prophet's voice cannot force life into a heart Allah has not opened. Other verses say the dead will hear a cry on the Day of Rising. That hearing is Allah's act, not the messenger's.\n\n\"Nor can you make the deaf hear the call when they turn their backs and walk away.\" A second picture: the deaf who also turn and leave. Two barriers, inner and outer. They do not hear, and they increase the distance. The call, \"al-du'a,\" is the invitation of the Quran. The Prophet is not blamed when such people walk off.\n\nThese images follow \"you are upon the clear truth\" so that he does not measure the truth by the number of listeners who stay. Clear truth can be spoken to backs. That does not stain the truth.\n\nThe stories already showed this limit. Moses came with enlightening signs. Hearts were convinced and still denied. Salih urged forgiveness. They spoke of omens. Lot rebuked. They voted expulsion. The Prophet of Mecca is in that line. Inability to force hearing is part of being a human messenger.\n\nA misconception is to stop inviting because some are \"dead.\" The verse limits power, not duty. He still says, still recites. He simply does not own the result. The next verse will add the blind and then state the exception: those who believe in the revelations and submit. The negative pictures prepare a positive remnant.\n\n'The dead' also sets the Quran's definition of life and death beyond pulse and breath. A person can trade, marry, argue, and lead while being counted among the dead, because the aliveness Allah speaks of here is the heart's responsiveness to truth. The formula cuts both ways with mercy: whoever turns is alive again at once, since the death being described is not burial but refusal.",
  "81": "\"Nor can you lead the blind out of their misguidance.\" A third picture joins the dead and the deaf: the blind. Misguidance, \"dalalah,\" is a lost road. The Prophet cannot take a person by the hand out of that lostness if the person will not see. Leading here is \"hadi,\" the kind of guiding that actually extracts someone from error. Ultimate extraction belongs to Allah.\n\n\"You can make none hear the truth except those who believe in Our revelations, fully submitting to Allah.\" After three impossibilities comes the one possibility. Hearing happens for those who believe in the signs and are \"muslimun,\" submitting. The same word closed Solomon's letter and the queen's confession. Submission is the hearing ear.\n\nThe verse does not flatter the Prophet with half a power. It gives him a precise field. His recitation reaches everyone physically. The hearing that changes a life belongs to believers who yield. That is why the Book is a guide and mercy \"for the believers\" a few verses ago.\n\nThe queen was not forced. She reasoned, tested, saw, and said she had wronged her soul. That is the path of those who come to hear. Pharaoh's people saw and called it magic. That is the path of the blind who remain blind.\n\nFor a caller in any age, the sentence prevents two errors: despair, because some will hear; and pride, because he did not pull the blind out by his own rope. The next verse will leap from this human limit to a sign of the Hour, a beast from the earth that will speak to people about their lack of sure faith. When human calling is refused long enough, another kind of address will come.\n\nThe exception clause also guards the messenger's effort from both pride and despair with one sentence. Those who hear are not his trophy; they believed in the revelations and submitted, and the words that reached them were Allah's. Those who walk away are not his failure; the extraction he cannot perform was never in his job. The verse leaves the preacher a precise peace: work inside the field given, and leave the seeing to the One who opened his own eyes first.",
  "82": "\"And when the decree of the Hour comes to pass against them\" moves from the Prophet's limited calling to a future sign. The \"qawl,\" the word or decree, falling upon them means the matter of the end is due, the time of a great sign has arrived. It is not the Prophet's schedule. It is Allah's.\n\n\"We will bring forth for them a beast from the earth.\" The \"dabba\" is a creature, a beast, coming out of the earth. Authentic reports from the Prophet mention a beast among the major signs of the Hour, speaking to people. The Quran here states the fact and the message. It does not describe the beast's shape, and later storytellers have added colors and forms that the Book does not require. What is required is to believe the sign as stated.\n\nThe beast will be \"telling them that the people had no sure faith in Our revelations.\" The message is a verdict on \"yaqin,\" sure faith. This chapter began by praising those who have sure faith in the Hereafter, and it blamed those who do not believe in it. The beast's speech returns to that opening. People lived without certainty in the signs. The creature of the earth will say so.\n\nThere is a striking reversal. Humans refused to hear a human prophet. A beast will speak, and the decree will already have come to pass. Hearing at that point is not the hearing of repentance that changes a nation's road in the usual way. It is a sign that the Hour's word has fallen.\n\nA misconception is to spend this verse on speculation and pictures. The verse's own center is the charge: no sure faith in the revelations. That is a charge a person can still answer today by taking the Quran as guide and mercy. The next verses leave the beast and show the gathering of deniers in ranks, the questioning, and the silence of those against whom the decree is justified.",
  "83": "\"Watch for the Day We will gather from every faith-community a group of those who denied Our revelations.\" After the beast comes a scene of gathering, \"nahshur.\" From every \"ummah,\" every community that had a message, a troop of deniers is collected. Denial of revelations is the shared crime across nations. Pharaoh's people, Thamud, Lot's town, and Mecca's mockers all fit the description.\n\n\"And they will be driven in ranks.\" The word \"yooza'oon\" was used earlier when Solomon's forces were perfectly organized. Here the same kind of ordering is applied to prisoners of the Day, held in rows, not allowed to scatter. The echo is bitter. Solomon's ranks were a gift of kingdom. These ranks are a herding to judgment.\n\n\"A group\" from every community does not mean only a few deniers existed. It pictures them brought as companies, representatives of a type, or waves of the larger mass of rejecters. The point is the worldwide, history-wide nature of the court. No community that denied is missing from the summons.\n\nThe Prophet who could not make the dead hear will see, on that Day, that hearing is no longer the issue. Gathering is the issue. The revelations they denied will be the file in front of them.\n\nThis verse should not be read as a reason to despair of whole nations now. It is a reason to take denial seriously. Communities have an end, and deniers within them have a rank in a march they cannot refuse. The next verse will put Allah's question in their faces: did you deny without even comprehending, or what exactly did you do?\n\n'Driven in ranks' also answers a habit of denial that relies on crowds. In this life, a mocker is strengthened by the company of mockers; the laugh of a whole assembly feels like evidence. The verse pictures the company arriving at court in formation, and the formation is no longer comfort. What felt like a majority on the street becomes a numbered unit in a file.",
  "84": "\"When they finally come before their Lord, He will ask them, 'Did you deny My revelations without even comprehending them?'\" The ranks arrive. The first question attacks a common method of denial: rejecting what one has not understood. \"Without even comprehending\" means they did not surround the signs with knowledge. They judged before study, mocked before thought, called the Quran a fable before they let it clarify anything.\n\n\"Or what exactly did you do?\" is the second fork. If they claim they did understand, then what was their work? Understanding plus denial is Pharaoh's crime in this chapter: hearts convinced, tongues arrogant. Understanding plus obedience is the believers' road. The question leaves them no third shelter. Either they denied in ignorance they chose, or they denied after knowing. Both are blameworthy in different ways.\n\nThe Lord says \"My revelations.\" The signs were His, not the Prophet's private verses. Denial was personal toward Allah.\n\nThis questioning matches the demand earlier: show me your proof if you are true. On that Day the proof they never had will be asked for again, when showing it is too late. The queen asked for time and sent a gift to learn. These companies refused to learn.\n\nThe verse is terrible in its calm. There is no shouting in the English line. There is a precise cross-examination. The next verse will say the decree of torment is justified and they are left speechless. The questions of this verse are why the mouth closes. When both ignorance and action have been exposed, speech has nowhere to stand.\n\n'Or what exactly did you do' also exposes the laziness hiding inside sophisticated doubt. Many deniers presented their refusal as intellectual modesty, a suspension of judgment; the question asks for the works that suspension produced and finds none. Honest doubt searches, questions, and tests, as the queen of Sheba tested. Doubt that only rests in mockery has already chosen, and the verse names the choice.",
  "85": "\"And the decree of torment will be justified against them for their wrongdoing.\" The \"qawl\" falls again, now as the justified sentence of punishment. \"Justified\" means it comes due because of \"zulm,\" their wrongdoing, not because of a random wrath. The questions have been asked. The file of denial without comprehension, or denial after knowledge, is complete. The word against them is deserved.\n\n\"Leaving them speechless.\" They \"la yantiqoon,\" they do not speak. Earlier they had many words: when will it come, ancient fables, you are a bad omen, expel the chaste, this is magic. The court takes the gift of speech away, or the terror and the proof take it away. There is nothing left that can help.\n\nSpeechless is the opposite of the beast that spoke and of the Quran that clarifies. Those who would not use the tongue for truth lose the tongue for defense. Other verses say their hands and feet will testify. This verse only needs their silence.\n\nThe Prophet was told he cannot make them hear. On this Day they cannot make themselves speak. The two limits frame a life of warning in between, which is the only time talk can still change an end.\n\nWrongdoing here gathers the chapter's crimes: associating partners, mocking resurrection, plotting murder, public shame, arrogant denial of signs. The decree is not a thin technicality. It is the moral sum. The next verse will suddenly point back to an everyday sign they can still see before that silence: night for rest and a bright day. Mercy still teaches while speech remains.\n\nTheir silence also completes a pattern the chapter drew in speech. The Quran clarifies, the beast is sent to speak to them, the Prophet recites; everything around them talks. They alone fall quiet, because speech was the gift they spent on mockery, and the balance is empty. The verse shows what a life's worth of wrong words costs at the counter where words matter.",
  "86": "\"Do they not see that We made the night for them to rest in and the day bright?\" Before the Trumpet of the next verse, the Quran pulls the eye to a sign that happens every twenty-four hours. Night is made \"to rest in,\" a mercy of stillness. Day is \"mubsiran,\" bright, making sight possible. The two are fashioned, not accidental.\n\n\"For them\" makes the gift personal. The same people who ask when the threat will come sleep under a night they did not invent and work under a day they cannot switch on.\n\n\"Surely in this are signs for those who believe.\" The signs are there for all eyes, but they function as signs for believers, as the Book functions as a guide for believers. Faith reads the pair of night and day as designed mercy. Denial sleeps and wakes as if owed the cycle.\n\nThe word \"see\" continues the chapter's habit of inviting sight: see the end of corruptors, see the ruined homes, travel and see, do they not see. Physical seeing of night and day should help the heart see the Maker.\n\nThis verse also softens the horror around it. A God who designs rest is not a God who only threatens. The people who will be speechless later were given nights of sleep in which they could have turned in repentance. Ingratitude, already named, includes treating rest as a right and dawn as a routine without praise.\n\nSolomon asked to do good that pleases his Lord. A believer can treat sleep and waking as a small daily submission. The next verse will show a Day when rest is gone and horror seizes the heavens and the earth at the Trumpet, except whom Allah wills to spare.\n\n'For them' also catches the sleeper in his own argument. The man who asks when the threat will come spent last night inside the mercy he refuses to credit, and will spend tonight inside it again. The verse does not shout about hellfire here; it mentions bedtime. Of all the chapter's signs, this is the one the audience has already used today, which makes the ingratitude measurable.",
  "87": "\"And beware of the Day the Trumpet will be blown\" sets a sound against the quiet of the night of rest. The \"sur,\" the Trumpet, is the signal of the Hour in many verses. When it is blown, ordinary cycles of sleep and work end.\n\n\"And all those in the heavens and all those on the earth will be horrified to the point of death, except those Allah wills to spare.\" The terror, \"faza',\" is universal among creatures. Angels, jinn, humans, the populations of sky and ground, are seized by it. The exception is real but unnamed here. Allah's will spares whom He wills. Commentators have mentioned angels nearest the Throne, or martyrs, or others. The text leaves the exception with Allah, which is safer than a list the Book did not lock.\n\n\"And all will come before Him, fully humbled.\" After horror comes attendance. \"Dakhirin\" means low, small, brought down. The arrogance of Pharaoh, the boasts of Sheba's chiefs about military might, the nine men's oath, the laugh about fables, all arrive as humility. They come; they are not invited as guests. They attend as subjects.\n\nThe verse gathers the whole cosmos, not one town. Earlier destructions were local rains and blasts. This Day is not local. That is why dust of fathers is no obstacle. The Trumpet that horrifies the heavens can raise the dust.\n\nFor the believer, the exception and the later promise of security from horror in the next verses become hope. For the denier, the picture answers \"when\" better than a date: when the Trumpet blows, dates will not matter. The next verse will add a visual shock: mountains the eye thought fixed will travel like clouds under the design of the One who perfected everything.\n\n'Except those Allah wills' also keeps the verse from being a wall of uniform terror. Within the universal shock, a mercy operates by the same will that ordered the shock, and the chapter immediately balances the scene with security for the people of good deeds. The picture is not meant to freeze the believer; it is meant to size the Day honestly, so that hope is anchored in the Judge rather than in the day's weather.",
  "88": "\"Now you see the mountains, thinking they are firmly fixed, but they are travelling just like clouds.\" The eye's habit is to treat mountains as the definition of stillness. The verse says that on that Day, or in the deeper reality Allah names, they move as clouds move. Clouds look slow and large and still from far away, yet they pass. Mountains will pass like that. The \"jamidah,\" the seeming solid, is not the last word.\n\nSome have also heard in this a hint that even now mountains are not the absolute fixtures people imagine. The verse's home, though, is the Day whose Trumpet was just blown, when the earth is rearranged. Other passages say mountains will be like plucked wool or scattered dust. Travelling like clouds belongs to that family of images.\n\n\"That is the design of Allah, Who has perfected everything.\" The movement is not chaos winning. It is \"sun' Allah,\" Allah's workmanship, the same perfection that made night for rest and day bright. He who perfected the still mountain can perfect its motion. \"Atqana\" means He made everything firm and well.\n\n\"Surely He is All-Aware of what you do.\" The One who handles mountains like clouds is not unaware of human deeds. The jump from geology to morality is intended. If your landmark can travel, your hidden act is not too small to be known.\n\nA person who asked how dust can rise is shown a greater wonder: the pegs of the earth on the move. The argument is by increase. The next verse will tell the frightened viewer what happens to the one who comes with a good deed: something better as reward, and security from the very horror the Trumpet caused.\n\n'Thinking' is the quiet word doing the argument. The verse does not say the mountains are fixed and will be unfixt; it says you see them that way, an observation about the seer as much as the seen. Throughout the chapter the mockers trusted what their eyes were used to, dust and stillness, and the verse gently relocates their certainty: permanence was never in the mountain, only in the habit of looking.",
  "89": "\"Whoever comes with a good deed will be rewarded with what is better.\" On the Day of horror and traveling mountains, the file is not only crimes. A person who comes with \"hasanah,\" a good deed, meets a reward better than the deed. Allah's fadl, His bounty already named, appears in payment. Ten times, or more, or paradise itself, have all been mentioned in the wider sunnah and tafsir as ways this \"better\" is fulfilled. The verse states the principle: good is not paid at a stingy rate.\n\n\"And they will be secure from the horror on that Day.\" The \"faza'\" that seized the heavens and the earth does not own the people of good. Security, \"aminun,\" is safety. Other verses say the greatest terror will not grieve them. This line makes the Trumpet scene livable for the believer.\n\nComing with a good deed means arriving with it as one's provision, a life that produced obedience, faith, and works. It is not a single coin tossed at the last second while the heart stayed a denier, though Allah may forgive as He wills. The chapter's own examples of good are sure faith, prayer, charity, thanks like Solomon's, submission like the queen's, and mindfulness like those delivered from Thamud.\n\nThe verse should not be turned into a claim that one tiny act buys the Day. It should be turned into hope that Allah's payment exceeds the work, and into fear that arriving empty is possible. The next verse will state the opposite arrival: whoever comes with an evil deed is hurled face-first into the Fire, and is only repaid for what he used to do. Better-than-the-deed for good, equal justice for evil, is a pairing that shows both bounty and fairness.\n\n'Comes with' also makes life a journey to an arrival, and the deed a luggage. The image shapes the believer's ordinary days: he is packing. Every prayer, every honest scale, every kindness to a parent is folded into what will be carried through a Day when mountains travel; and the promise is that the Carrier of the load is more generous than the load is heavy.",
  "90": "\"And whoever comes with an evil deed will be hurled face-first into the Fire.\" The pair to the previous verse is now spoken. A good deed arrived to a better reward and to safety from horror. An evil deed, \"sayyi'ah,\" arrives to a throw. \"Hurled face-first\" is the English of being overturned on the faces into the Fire. The face, which once wore mockery and pride, meets the ground of punishment first.\n\n\"Are you rewarded except for what you used to do?\" closes the door of complaint. The payment matches the habit, \"ma kuntum ta'maloon,\" what you used to work. No one is burned for a life he did not live. The question form makes the damned admit the justice even if they cannot speak a defense, as an earlier verse left them speechless.\n\nEvil deed here is not only one isolated slip of a believer who still comes with faith. In the Quran's court scenes, coming with the sayyi'ah often means arriving with unbelief and the works that grew from it, the sum of a refused guidance. Believers who sin and repent are treated in other verses with forgiveness. This verse is the dark twin of \"whoever comes with a good deed.\"\n\nThe throwing on the faces answers the chiefs who boasted of strength and the nine who planned by night and the people who expelled the chaste. Their chosen postures of power become a posture of fall.\n\nTogether with verse eighty-nine, the chapter's moral math is complete: better than the good, and not more than the evil. Bounty on one side, pure justice on the other. The remaining verses will send the Prophet back to his mission in Mecca: worship, recite, warn, and praise, while Allah shows signs and is never unaware.\n\n'Face-first' also mirrors a lifetime of direction. The proud face pointed upward by choice, refusing to lower itself before anyone; the fall simply completes the geometry, turning the face toward the lowest place. The Quran's punishments often answer sins in kind like this, and readers have long noticed the fittingness: the sentence is not inventive cruelty but the deed's own shape arriving at its end.",
  "91": "\"Say, O Prophet, 'I have only been commanded to worship the Lord of this city of Mecca.'\" The stories and the Hour now fold back into a personal commission. The Prophet is to declare a limit and a center. He is not commanded to invent a kingdom like Solomon's. He is commanded to worship. The Lord he worships is \"the Lord of this city,\" Mecca, the town of the Kaaba, the town that is persecuting him.\n\n\"Who has made it sacred\" reminds the Quraysh of a gift they love. Mecca is \"harramaha,\" made inviolable, a sanctuary where bloodshed and hunting were restricted, a place of pilgrimage that fed their honor and their trade. That sacredness is not from their idols. It is from the Lord of the city.\n\n\"And to Him belongs everything\" widens the claim from one town to all things. The Lord of Mecca is not a local god. The phrase stops anyone from hearing \"Lord of this city\" as a smaller title.\n\n\"And I am commanded to be one of those who fully submit to Him.\" Again \"muslimin,\" the same word of Solomon's letter and the queen's last sentence. The Prophet of Mecca stands in that line. His religion is submission, not a new tribal cult.\n\nThe verse is an answer to those who said he sought power or foreign tales. His \"only\" is worship and belonging to the submitters. Recitation will be added in the next verse. Together they are the mission. The sacred city that rejects him is still a proof for him, because its sacredness came from the God he calls them to. To love Mecca's sanctuary and deny its Lord is a contradiction the sentence exposes.\n\n'This city' also plants the mission in the hardest soil on purpose. A prophet might have preferred any audience but the neighbours who mock him, and the verse names those neighbours' own town as the center of his commanded worship. Holiness, the sentence implies, is not a refuge from hostile people; it is an assignment among them, held in place by the Lord who hallowed the ground.",
  "92": "\"And to recite the Quran.\" The command that began with worship continues with recitation. The Prophet's public work is to recite this Book that the chapter called clear, a guide, a mercy, a clarifier of differences. He is not told to force results. He is told to make the words heard.\n\n\"Then whoever chooses to be guided, it is only for their own good.\" Guidance chosen is a benefit that returns to the guided, as Solomon said gratitude is for the grateful person's own good. Allah does not need their guided state. They need it.\n\n\"But whoever chooses to stray, say, O Prophet, 'I am only a warner.'\" Straying is also a choice in this wording. The Prophet's response to that choice is a title: \"I am only a warner.\" He is not a controller of hearts, as the verses about the dead, the deaf, and the blind already said. A warner delivers news of danger and of promise. He does not own the road after that.\n\nThe pairing of chosen guidance and chosen straying respects human response without making man the creator of guidance in Allah's independent sense. Other verses say Allah guides whom He wills. This verse speaks from the side of human choosing and responsibility. Both sides are Quranic. Here the stress is that the Prophet's back is not broken by their choice.\n\nThe chapter began as a guide and good news for believers. It ends with the reciter who cannot make everyone into a believer. That honesty is part of why the Book is trusted. It does not pretend a messenger is a magician of souls. The last verse will put praise on his tongue and promise that signs will be shown and recognized, while the Lord remains aware of what they do.\n\n'I am only a warner' also calibrates the messenger's title at exactly the moment tempers run hottest. After choices diverge, the temptation is to chase the strayer or to gloat over him, and the verse hands the Prophet a role small enough to carry: warn, recite, let go. The word 'only,' far from being a demotion, is the secret of his stamina across years of mixed reception.",
  "93": "\"And say, 'All praise is for Allah!'\" The chapter that told the Prophet to say praise after the stories of the chosen servants now ends on praise again. \"Al-hamd\" is the right close after commands to worship, recite, and warn. The messenger's last public word in this surah is not a curse. It is praise.\n\n\"He will show you His signs, and you will recognize them.\" Signs already filled the chapter: a staff, a shining hand, an ant's speech, a throne in a blink, crystal like water, ruined homes, a rain of brimstone, night and day, traveling mountains. More signs will be shown in the future of the hearers, in the world and in their own selves, until recognition is unavoidable. \"Fa-ta'rifunaha,\" you will know them as what they are.\n\n\"And your Lord is never unaware of what you do.\" The last clause answers every secret plot, every quiet mockery, every hidden doubt, and every unseen good. Unawareness is denied. The Record mentioned earlier, the knowledge of what chests conceal, the All-Aware who sees deeds while mountains move—all of that is gathered into one simple close.\n\nThe queen recognized a disguised throne. This verse says people will recognize Allah's signs. Recognition can come in time for submission, as it did for her, or too late, as it did for those left speechless. The promise that signs will be shown is therefore both mercy and warning.\n\nSo the chapter of the ant ends where a clear Book should end: praise, coming signs, and a Lord who does not sleep on human action. The next chapter will open with similar letters and another telling of Moses, as if the reciter is immediately given more of the story he was commanded to recite.\n\n'You will recognize them' also promises recognition rather than mere sight, and the difference is the whole story of this chapter. The queen looked at an altered throne and recognised it; Pharaoh's court looked at clear signs and called them magic. The same signs, two different hearts. The verse promises a day when the seeing itself will settle, and asks, gently, that the listener not wait until then to start."
};
